D-1 - Procedure to action a telephony remission request.

Last published 6 July 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

D-1

This page contains the procedure for actioning a telephony remission or cancellation request for:

general interest charge (GIC)
failure to lodge on time (FTL) penalty
shortfall interest charge (SIC).
Information

For correspondence requests, including internal and escalated telephony requests, go to Correspondence request - GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation.
Opens in a new window
 

Warning

Staff from FS (excluding specialised taxpayer support and debt large) can no longer assess telephony remission requests:

from legal practitioners and registered agents, when access to OSfA is not possible
over $2,500 for each penalty and interest type, for all other taxpayers.

These must be escalated to the dedicated remissions team with a completed Telephony remission application.

A Telephony remission application form must be completed for:

GIC - Telephony GIC remission application form
Opens in a new window
FTL - Telephony FTL remission application form
Opens in a new window
SIC - Telephony SIC remission application form.
Opens in a new window

To complete the form, a copy of the form must be downloaded. Select:

File from menu ribbon on the opened form
Create a copy from the drop-down menu
Download a copy.

Open the downloaded copy, select enable editing and complete. If assistance is needed, consult your support officer.

Note:

We are progressively making debts that were placed on hold before November 2023 visible in account balances. When these debts are included, GIC will be remitted by the system for:
the period the debts on hold were not included in the account balance, and
a further 6 months fixed term from the date the debt on hold is included in account balance.
For more information, refer to Including debts on hold in account balance to make them visible in Debts on hold (uneconomical non-pursued debt).
Opens in a new window
Intention

We want the public to have confidence in the way we:

ensure the tax and superannuation systems are fair for everyone, and
address non-compliance.

We use available tools and information to encourage taxpayers to willingly participate. We can:

apply interest charges
administer penalties fairly by
remit (partially or in full) interest charges and penalties in certain situations under the law

Full or partial payment of outstanding primary tax is not mandatory for consideration of remission requests.

