Procedure Steps

ABN holders impacted by high fuel costs after 30 June 2026

If an ABN holder meets all of the following criteria, take this into consideration and apply flexibility in negotiating payment of the debt.

They have experienced an increase in business operating costs and these costs are either: 

directly attributable to higher fuel costs, or
indirectly attributable to high fuel costs because of increased transport, logistics or other supply chain costs.

Additionally, they:

have a new tax debt, or they are unable to service an existing tax debt
can demonstrate a reduced capacity to pay due to the high fuel prices. This is separate from a general downturn in business or ordinary cashflow issues. This means that if fuel prices hadn’t been so high, they could have been able to meet their payment obligations, including instalments under existing payment plans.

Flexibility can include:

reducing or removing upfront payment requirements
allowing payment plans over a longer period
when undertaking pre-serviceability checks, consider the required upfront payment, plan duration, and likelihood of payment using the 24 Months Metrics Data Driven Insights for Payment Plans gadget in ECP with flexibility applied where appropriate.

