Advise taxpayer of payment plan conditions, plus additional conditions for direct debit and director penalty liability.

Last published 23 June 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page provides the:

conditions to advise the taxpayer after

providing a payout figure for an existing plan, or

granting a payment plan, extension of time to pay (extension) or payment in full (PIF)

additional conditions for direct debit and director penalty liability.

Ensure the taxpayer understands all conditions relevant to the agreed payment plan. Provide additional conditions under the headings for direct debit and director penalty liability if applicable.
Recent updates
23/06/2026	Update to 'Extension/PIF'.	SD-104917
Self Help
ATO website
Help the taxpayer to go to the page while on the phone.

This is a chance to educate the taxpayer about self-help options, which may lead to:

the taxpayer using self-service in the future, and
a better taxpayer experience.

Taxpayers can obtain more information from the ATO website on:

Payment plan conditions
Opens in a new window

Request a direct debit.
Opens in a new window

Use our self-service any time - it's easy, free and fast. Refer to Self-help services.
Opens in a new window

In Detail
Payment processing times

Payments:

need to reach us on or before its due date
can take up to 4 business days to be received by us and to appear on the taxpayers ATO account
made on a weekend, Australian national public holiday or after the processing cut off time on business days, will be processed on the next business day.
Standard/review plan
Taxpayers can pay the debt in full or make additional payments at any time. They can check their account balance via online services or by calling 13 11 42 for a payout figure.
Taxpayers need to allow for payment processing times to ensure payments are received by the ATO on or before the due date.
If instalments and ongoing obligations are not lodged and paid in full by the due date

the payment plan may default
penalties may apply.
If any tax credits arise, they

may be offset against any existing debt
may reduce the length of the payment plan
