We are making changes to ensure taxpayers can see their debts placed on hold before November 2023. These debts on hold will be included in the account balance. Refer to Including debts on hold in account balance to make them visible.

This page contains information about debts on hold (also known as uneconomical non-pursuits), including:

how to locate them in accounts
determining if offsetting or re-raising has occurred
identifying if debts on hold have been included in the account balance as part of visibility, and
providing explanations to taxpayers.

A debt on hold is a debt we have paused taking actions to collect. We may place a debt on hold if we decide it is not cost effective to collect the debt at the time.

The debt remains legally due and payable and general interest charge (GIC) may apply regardless of the age of the debt.

There are currently 2 types of system treatments for debts on hold:

‘Non-pursuit - uneconomical to pursue' transaction (debts placed on hold before November 2023).
When these were actioned the debt amount was removed from the account balance.
The debt amounts cannot be seen in the account balance, in ICP or ATO online services.
Refer to Including debts on hold in account balance to make them visible.
‘Non pursuable debt’ accounting treatment (debts placed on hold after November 2023).
These remain included in the account balance. In most cases:
these are placed on hold by the system
taxpayers are not aware their debt is on hold
there is no visible sign externally that the debt is on hold.
This is not a long-term arrangement as debts can be taken off hold at any time based on the taxpayer’s circumstances.

Note: Placing a debt on hold is not the preferred treatment for taxpayers experiencing severe hardship or vulnerability.

