How to support taxpayers that have an existing payment plan when their debt on hold is included

Some taxpayers have one or more payment plans that do not include their debt on hold amounts. This may lead to confusion for taxpayers when we include their debt on hold as they may think the payment plan includes all the taxes they owe.

When we include the debt on hold in the account balance, the taxpayer will have 6 months to pay the amount in full or enter a new payment plan before it impacts the payment plan.

You can confirm these taxpayers by looking on the Siebel Internal Initiated Action (IIA) tab, and locating the ‘Debt on hold included and existing payment plan’ note.

What to do if the taxpayer calls

If a taxpayer calls, make sure you listen to their concerns and where required:

discuss the assessment that raised the debts on hold
explain the impact on their payment plan
offer support.

Taxpayer scenarios

The following scenarios are the most common.

Scenario 1: the debt on hold amount can be paid in full or the existing payment plan ends within 6 months - no changes needed to the existing payment plan.

Scenario 2: the taxpayer cannot pay the debt on hold amount and requests a new payment plan - if the debt on hold is on:

the same account as the payment plan
cancel
