enter a new payment plan to include all the debt
a different account to the payment plan
no changes needed to existing payment plan
set up a new payment plan for the debt on hold amount only.
What to say if the taxpayer questions if the debt is real or new

Yes, the debt is a real assessment, and in most cases, these were reported by the taxpayer (self-reported). If assessments were ATO initiated, the taxpayer was notified of the amount owing at the time it was assessed.

Some debts may be a result of penalties or interest. The taxpayer can see what the debt relates to in their Review your account balance letter and statement of account. Remember taxpayers may have thought the debt went away or forgotten that these debts exist so make sure you provide a tailored account explanation to help them understand.

What to advise if the taxpayer disputes or objects to the debt

If a taxpayer disagrees with the relevant assessment or the amendment assessment that raised the debt, they will need to lodge an objection. They cannot object to the:

debt amount (they need to object to the assessment)
debt being placed on hold, or
debt being taken off hold.

Keep in mind that in most cases for a debt that was placed on hold before November 2023, the standard time limit to lodge an objection may have passed. The taxpayer can still lodge an objection even though it is outside the time limit.

In the first instance refer the taxpayer to Object to a decision
Opens in a new window
 on the ATO website so they can self-serve. If the taxpayer requires further support refer to Objections.
Opens in a new window

How long will the debt stay on hold

When the debt is included in the account balance it will be placed back on hold. This is to provide the taxpayer time to understand their debt position or seek support if needed.

If the taxpayer’s situation changes and we believe it is cost effective to collect their debt on hold, we may take it off hold. The debt may be taken off hold, however recovery actions will continue to be paused until after 30 June 2026. To identify the status of a debt, refer to Identify a debt on hold.

We do not contact a taxpayer to let them know their debt has come off hold, but we may contact them to discuss paying their debt.

Each taxpayer’s circumstances are different therefore an exact timeframe of how long a debt will remain on hold cannot be given.

Refer to Identify a debt on hold to help you determine if a debt is still on hold.

When do I need to pay

An automatic GIC free period of 6 months has been given to allow the taxpayer time to address their debt. The purpose of the letter is to remind taxpayers that this debt still exists and is now included in the account balance. It is not a demand for immediate payment and does not include payment reference numbers. Taxpayers can pay in full or make payments through ATO online services.

Staff can recommend the taxpayer to make contributions to pay their debt amount in full before the GIC free period ends.

Proceed to discuss payment options it the taxpayer wants to continue.

What to say if a taxpayer asks why they were not told about their debt on hold until now

When a debt was placed on hold, to stop recovery actions it had to be removed from the account balance. This made it confusing for some taxpayers who may have believed their debt no longer existed. Additionally, our communication in the past may have been inadequate or caused some confusion.

Before we took any steps to include these debts on hold in account balances, we reviewed our approach to improve our communication and practices. For example, to explain the debt and where it comes from, we now include the origin of the debt in our communications issued to impacted taxpayers.

