What to say if the debt on hold relates to PAYGI or penalties and interest

If the debt relates to PAYGI, make sure the income tax returns are lodged.

Generally, if the tax returns have been lodged then the PAYGI credits should be included in the notice of assessment for the relevant year.

If the tax returns are not lodged, explain to the taxpayer that they will need to bring them up to date and the credits will be applied for those relevant years. Make sure you tailor the information to the taxpayer's account. For further information refer to PAYG instalments.
Opens in a new window

If the debt relates to penalties and interest, explain to the taxpayer that they may have incurred interest or penalties on the account before the debt was placed on hold.

Failure to lodge (FTL) penalties don’t display as ‘FTL’ in the ‘Review your account balance’ letter or transaction name because they don’t have their own role. Rather, they link to the tax type of the late or not lodged obligation, such as income tax or activity statements.

Provide a tailored account explanation to help the taxpayer understand. During the taxpayer interaction, if they have provided reasons to consider a remission, refer to GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation request.
Opens in a new window

If a taxpayer did not need to lodge, help them update their records and follow the Action non-lodgment advice.
