Which debts placed on hold before 1 January 2017 are being included

Proposed changes announced in the 2024–25 Budget measure (not yet law) will give us discretion not to offset in certain circumstances. The proposed changes are expected to affect some debts placed on hold before 1 January 2017.

The ATO is currently excluding taxpayers with debts placed on hold before 1 January 2017 for individuals, small business, and not-for-profit taxpayer segments from visibility actions.

However, the proposed law does not apply to debts of large businesses, super funds, multinationals, and taxpayers viewed as privately owned and wealthy groups (POWG). 

The ATO views POWGs as:

Australian resident individuals who, together with their business associates, control net wealth over $5 million, and/or
companies and their associated subsidiaries (often referred to as economic groups) with an annual turnover of more than $10 million and that are not public groups or foreign owned.

These debts on hold will be included in account balances to make them visible.

