Offsetting and re-raises (including where's my refund)

When debts are on hold, the ATO will not contact taxpayers to pay the debt but will use any credits or refunds to reduce it. This is called offsetting
Opens in a new window
 and it is required by law.

In the past, the ATO excluded some debts from offsetting such as for taxpayers on low incomes. However, the Australian Government Solicitor advised this was not in line with the law and so offsetting recommenced. 

The process to re-raise is different depending on the system treatment for the debt on hold. There are 2:

‘Non pursuit’ transaction, and
‘Non pursuable debt’ accounting treatment.
‘Non pursuit’ transaction

When there is an offsetting event for a debt on hold that is not currently included in the account balance, it will be re-raised either in part or in full to the credit amount available. Staff can re-raise debts on hold in full at a taxpayer's request. For more information, refer to Non-pursuit re-raise (Frontline Compliance and Debt Assist).

When explaining a ‘re-raise’ to taxpayers ensure you use language the taxpayer will understand. Refer to these transactions as an existing debt included in the account balance.

Staff and taxpayers can identify if a debt on hold has been re-raised and offset. Identify the following transaction descriptions on the SOAs in ICP and ATO online services:

existing debt on hold included in account balance
re-raise of non-pursuit amount
partial re-raise of non-pursuit amount.

Note: The transaction description ‘existing debt on hold included in the account balance’ can be either a part or full re-raise.

‘Non pursuable debt’ accounting treatment

When a debt is placed on hold and remains in the account balance offsetting will automatically occur.

