Paying a debt on hold

When a debt is on hold, it remains legally due and payable and general interest charge (GIC) may apply regardless of the age of the debt.

When the decision has been made to place a debt on hold, it removes the taxpayer from our Payment Receivables Management (PRM) system. This means any collection actions stop while the debt remains on hold. PRM is the system the ATO uses to determine the next best actions for a taxpayer with overdue lodgments or debt.

The process to pay a debt on hold is different depending on the system treatment.

There are 2: ‘Non pursuit’ transaction, and ‘Non pursuable debt’ accounting treatment.

Before acting or providing payment information to taxpayers, you must determine the type of debt on hold system treatment. If you need help understanding the difference, refer to Identify a debt on hold.

‘Non pursuit’ transaction

This debt on hold will not be included in the account balance. Taxpayers must contact the ATO to ‘re-raise’ the amount.

Note: When explaining a ‘re-raise’ to taxpayers, ensure you use language the taxpayer will understand. Refer to these transactions as an existing debt included in the account balance.

‘Non pursuable debt’ accounting treatment

As the debt remained in the account balance when it was placed on hold, taxpayers can pay part or all of their debt including setting up a payment plan via ATO online services.

FAQs

What to do if a taxpayer disagrees with a debt on hold

What to do If a taxpayer requests permanent removal of the debt on hold amount

What to do if the taxpayer questions the general interest charge (GIC) calculations

What to do if the taxpayer believes their debt on hold was ‘written-off’ or no longer payable

What to do if the taxpayer is expressing hardship or vulnerability

What to do if a taxpayer disagrees with a debt on hold

A taxpayer cannot object to a decision to place a debt on hold or take it off hold.

If a taxpayer disagrees with the debt they can object to the relevant assessment or amended assessment that raised the debt. To do this they will need to lodge an objection.
Opens in a new window

What to do If a taxpayer requests permanent removal of the debt on hold amount

In limited circumstances, the ATO can waive debts. For example, certain debts where payment would result in serious hardship. Refer the taxpayer to Release from your tax debt.
Opens in a new window

What to do if the taxpayer questions the general interest charge (GIC) calculations

For debts on hold with ‘Non pursuit’ transaction system treatment, GIC will be applied when the debt is re-raised. However, in recognition that the taxpayer was unable to see the debt on hold, the decision was made to remit the GIC that accrued during the time it was not included in the account balance. We do this by applying the 0% Concessional GIC accounting treatment.
Opens in a new window

Note: These taxpayers may have GIC that was applied to their debt before it was placed on hold and removed from the account balance.

Debts on hold with the ‘Non pursuable debt’ accounting treatment remain in the account balance and therefore GIC is applicable.

For both system treatments, if the taxpayer asks to have GIC remitted refer to GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation request.
Opens in a new window

What to do if the taxpayer believes their debt on hold was ‘written-off’ or no longer payable

Confirm there are debt on hold amounts using the ECP
Opens in a new window
 Debt Non-pursuit Amounts gadget. Ensure the amounts are not irrecoverable at law or released.

If there is a debt on hold amount remaining, it is important that staff explain:

due to system limitations at the time, to prevent legal actions, debts on hold were removed from the account balance
in previous years the ATO referred to a debt that was placed on hold as ‘written off’. This may have caused some confusion in the community as they believed written off meant that their debt was permanently removed
the debt on hold has remained legally due and payable regardless of its age.
What to do if the taxpayer is expressing hardship or vulnerability

Always be empathetic and listen to the taxpayer and tailor the conversation to suit their circumstances.

When the decision has been made to place a debt on hold, we pause taking actions to collect it, however the debt remains due and legally payable. The ATO will continue to use any credits or refunds a taxpayer becomes entitled to, to reduce the debt on hold. This is called offsetting.

If the taxpayer requests not to offset the debt on hold, in limited circumstances the Commissioner has the discretion not to offset and to instead issue a refund, refer the taxpayer to PS LA 2011/21.
Opens in a new window

Generally, a debt on hold does not fit the eligibility for the Commissioner’s discretion, refer to Offsetting
Opens in a new window
 for more information.

Hardship - If you have identified a taxpayer is experiencing financial difficulties or serious financial hardship as per Taxpayer facing financial difficulties or serious hardship
Opens in a new window
 warm transfer to the Hardship team x92176. If taxpayer has disclosed immediate vulnerability, please follow vulnerability escalation below. 

Vulnerability - If you have identified a taxpayer is experiencing immediate and severe vulnerability factors that significantly impacts their capacity to engage, lodge or pay (for example, domestic and family violence, coercion, threats of self-harm) as per Tailored support for taxpayers experiencing vulnerability,
Opens in a new window
 warm transfer to Specialised Client Support on x92196.

