ECA for lodgment

From March 2020, the ATO does not use ECA for collection of outstanding lodgments.

How we work with ECA
We do not pay commissions.
We pay the agency a flat fee for service, per case, regardless of the amount recovered.
We do not ‘sell’ the overdue obligations to ECAs. It always remains collectable to us.
The referral of a debt to ECA does not affect a taxpayer’s credit rating.
ECAs will have access to ATO systems to view the taxpayer’s record and update contact details.
ECAs do not undertake legal action, however they do provide legal warnings where appropriate.
Cases we refer to ECA

Cases between $200 to $350,000 may be considered for referral. ECA have delegation to manage multiple accounts with a maximum debt amount of up to $650,000 across all referred accounts. These cases include:

activity statement debts
income tax debts
superannuation guarantee charge (SGC) debts.

In some instances, a new debt amount may arise while the case is being managed by ECA.

This new debt may be considered for referral once the debt has become due and payable. The agency must advise the taxpayer of the new debt.

Only active due and payable debt is referred to the ECA. Debts that have been non-pursued will not be referred to the ECA.

