Rules about TFNs
What if the legality of the external referral is questioned?

If a taxpayer questions why an external debt collection agency has access to their TFN, provide them with the following information:

tax law enables the Commissioner to disclose information to third parties where they have been appointed to carry out a duty or function under a tax law. Collection of taxation debts is one such duty
the ATO has a contract in place with the agency to recover tax debts for the Commonwealth. The ATO is entitled to provide taxpayer information to the agency for the purpose of collecting tax debts
the agencies must sign a secrecy declaration. This is to acknowledge their obligations as officers under all taxation law secrecy provisions. They are also contractually bound to
safeguard the privacy of taxpayer information including TFNs, and
use the information supplied by the ATO for no other purpose than to collect the debt on the ATO's behalf.

This information is in accordance with the Taxation Administration Act 1953 (TAA), the Income Tax Assessment Act 1936 (ITAA 1936) and the Privacy Act 1988 (Privacy Act).

Rules about TFNs  

There are special rules about TFNs in the TAA.

Subsection...	 Of the TAA...
8WB(1)
Opens in a new window
Opens in a new window
	

states a person must not:

record another person's TFN or maintain such a record
use another person's TFN in a manner connecting it with the other person's identity, or
divulge or communicate another person's TFN to a third person.

8WB(1A)
Opens in a new window
Opens in a new window
	

provides exceptions to these rules. It states that subsection 8WB(1) does not apply: 

to the extent required or permitted by, or reasonably necessary in order to comply with an obligation imposed by a taxation law 
in connection with the first-mentioned person 
exercising powers or performing functions under, or in relation to, a taxation law, or 
acting on the other person's behalf in the conduct of the other person's affairs.

8WB(1A)(b)
Opens in a new window
	

allows the ATO to use TFN information to:

exercise a power, and/or
perform a function under a taxation law.

The Australian Privacy Principles (APP) in section 14
Opens in a new window
Opens in a new window
 of the Privacy Act also governs the use and disclosure of TFNs. APP 6 concerns the use or disclosure of personal information.

These obligations are in addition to the TAA requirements. Recording a third party's details is an acceptable use of personal information under APP 6. This includes there:

TFN in accordance with the TAA, which is authorised by law for the purposes of the Privacy Act
full name, address or date of birth (DOB), which is used for
the same purpose the information was originally collected, or
at least for a directly related purpose.

This information:

generally, should not be disclosed to any person, body or agency (other than the individual concerned)
may be disclosed under APP 6. For more information, go to Handling of information and refer to Privacy.
Handling of information
ECA possessing up-to-date information
Privacy
Secrecy
Securing taxpayer information with the ECA

ECA possessing up-to-date information

ECAs have access to ATO systems which enable them to have up-to-date account information whilst being able to update taxpayers contact information. This complements taxpayer information sent by the ATO as part of the referral process and allows better informed discussion with the taxpayer.

Privacy

Tax law enables the Commissioner to disclose information to third parties where those third parties have been appointed to carry out a duty or function under a tax law. Collection of taxation debts is one such duty.

The Privacy Act 1988 requires the ATO to take contractual measures with the contracted service providers. The measures are to ensure they do not breach Australian Privacy Principles (APP) in the Privacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012.

The contracts with the external debt collection agencies bind them to act with the APP. This includes complying with APP 6. APP6 binds them not to use or disclose any personal information they receive under the contract for another purpose.

Secrecy

Division 355 of Schedule 1
Opens in a new window
 of the Taxation Administration Act 1953 (TAA 1953) outlines the secrecy provisions. Section 355-15 of Schedule 1 provides who the provisions apply to.

The ATO requires all Contractors and Contractor Personnel to sign a secrecy declaration acknowledging their obligations as officers under all taxation law secrecy provisions, and further we have done this in the case of referrals to external debt collection agencies.

An 'in-house' officer will be able to communicate Taxpayer information to the Contractor or Contractor Personnel for the purposes of enabling them to perform services on behalf of the Commissioner either in the performance of their duties (under section 350-50) which enables the disclosure of information to 'any entity for the purpose of enabling the entity to understand or comply with its obligations under a taxation law.'

For more information, see If you don’t pay.
Opens in a new window

Securing taxpayer information with the ECA

The contract between ATO and the ECA oblige them to meet all Commonwealth privacy and security requirements. ECAs must not use ATO data, or any information obtained as part of the provision of the Debt Management Services for any other purpose than the collection of tax liabilities.

ECAs operate as an extension of the ATO, and all operations are based in Australia. All information provided is encrypted and held on separate secure servers that meet the Commonwealth information security standards to the required classification level. ECAs must comply with all applicable Australian Laws.

Whole of taxpayer
Do collection agencies action whole of taxpayer?

ECA actions are limited to negotiation for outstanding obligations, they are unable to perform regular whole of taxpayer actions such as portal messages, Client initiated correspondence etc.

They are unable to follow whole of taxpayer procedures as they do not have authority to access all of ATO systems.

