Payment plans
Warning

Entering a payment plan into ICP will withdraw the taxpayer from the ECA.

The ECA may call the FSS to obtain approval or refusal for an arrangement which is outside of their delegation.
Opens in a new window
 Refer to Actioning ECA Payment Plan Approvals/refusals below.

The ECA may call the FSS to obtain technical assistance in managing a payment negotiation or outcome with the taxpayer. The system in which the ECA will need to update depends on the payment method the taxpayer chooses to use.

Payment methods

Direct Debit - Bank account and credit/debit card

ECA debt officers have been given the authority to enter direct debit via bank account and credit/debit card payment plans into ATO systems (ICP). These plans will be visible in ICP and managed by the ATO going forward.

ECA Debt officer will:

create a Siebel activity to record their payment plan notes and actions
enter payment plan into ICP (with a Status of 'Arr-Pending' if approval required)
issue direct debit agreement letter to the taxpayer in line with Direct debit - Issue service agreement letter.
Opens in a new window

If you are contacted by an ECA debt officer requesting for the FSS to enter a direct debit via bank account arrangement into ICP, advise the debt officer:

they are able to enter this payment plan into ICP themselves as per their Debt Management Services Guidelines
Opens in a new window
 (DMSG)
that once the plan has been input into ICP, the taxpayer will be withdrawn from the ECA and that retrieval can take up to 14 days. 
All other payment methods

ECA debt officer enters payment plans for all other payment methods into their own systems, as a result:

these payment plans will not be visible to the ATO in ICP
payment instalments will however be visible in the taxpayer’s statement of account (SOA). ECA debt officers cannot enter a payment plan into ICP. They must use their own systems to input negotiated payments plans (with the exception of direct debit).

Actioning ECA Payment Plan Approvals/refusals

Step
	Action


1

	

