Procedure Steps
Review the request

Review the taxpayer's account before starting the calculation. All of the taxpayer's circumstances must be taken into account to avoid an incorrect remission outcome.

Step	Action


1

	

Identify where the amendments are applied on the taxpayer's account.

Check if the account was in credit prior to the first amendment. If:

yes, go to step 2

no, go to step 3.




2

	

Identify the source of the credit.

Make a note of the following details:

the amount of the credit
the date of the credit.

Note: Credit details need to be applied to the calculation below in order to stop us over remitting GIC on the account.




3

	

As part of the amendments made by Compliance, check if there were also any credit amendments made. If:

yes, go to step 4
no, go to step 5.



4

	

Identify the amount of the credits and the effective dates as these will be used as part of the calculation below.




5

	

Check if there are any accounting treatments that may impact the application of GIC on the amendments (but not the Health Accounting treatment applied due the impact of COVID - see above). If:

yes, go to step 6
no, go to step 7.



6

	

Make a note of the start and end date of the accounting treatment. This will need to be applied to the calculation below.




7

	

Check if there are any GIC amounts applied post the amendments, that result in a debit and credit being applied for a particular period. If:

yes, go to step 8
no, go to step 9.



8

	

Make a note of the period for which GIC was subsequently applied and remitted. This will need to be applied to each worksheet below.




9

	

Go to Calculate remission for a shortfall.

Calculate remission for a shortfall
Step	Action


1

	

Go to BDA hub
Opens in a new window
 and install the GIC SIC Base Rate calculator macro. The calculator will launch after installation.

If previously installed, launch the calculator.




2

	

Enter the shortfall amount (as per the remission request) in the Tax Shortfall field in column I, highlighted in blue.




3

	

Work out the Start date by identifying the effective date of the liability. GIC is applicable on the effective date if the amount is not paid on the effective date.

It is important to check the date that the amended GIC was applicable. In some instances GIC may be amended (and calculated) from a date after the effective date of the activity statement. For example, if Audit amends an activity statement that was:

originally a credit, and then results in a refund
GIC would start from the date of the refund, not the original date of the activity statement.

In column B row 9, enter the above Start date and press Enter.

The spreadsheet will populate all subsequent quarters up to yesterday's date.




4

	

Check if effective AS payment due date is before the From date under the remit to base column on the attached calculator. If:

yes, go to step 6

no (that is, on or after the From date), go to step 5.




5

	

In column C, find the quarter which the End date of the remission request period falls within.

Replace the quarter End date with the End date. This date will be highlighted in red and that quarter is split into 2 separate rows.

Go to step 9.




6

	

In column C, find the quarter which the From date of the remission request period falls within.

Replace the quarter End date with the From date minus one day. This date will be highlighted in red and that quarter is split into 2 separate rows.

The spreadsheet will then self-populate the row below with the remission From date as per the referral request.




7

	

In column D, select the code GIC in all rows up to the red End date.




8

	

In column C, find the quarter which the To date of the remission request period falls within.

Replace the quarter End date with the To date of the remission request. This date will be highlighted in red and that quarter is split into 2 separate rows.




9

	

If there are more remission periods for the same shortfall, repeat steps 6, 7, and 8 for all remission periods.

Delete all periods after the final remission by:

going to the row below the last red End date

deleting the start date in column B of the above row. This will automatically remove all periods after the final remission.

Remit GIC
Step	Action


1

	

Check if the remission request is to remit in full (Code: Zero) or to the base rate (Code: Base).

In column D, select the relevant Code in the row below the first red End date and all subsequent rows up to and including the second red End date.

All remaining rows (below the last 'red date') will need to be updated to 'GIC' (as it defaults to 'SIC'). The last date should be today's date.




2

	

Apply any updates in chronological order (i.e. in the order in which the events occurred, from first to last), based on the effective date, to your calculation spreadsheets as identified in Review the request.

Examples of these updates include:

Applying a credit or payment
Opens in a new window

Credit amendment
Opens in a new window

Accounting treatment
Opens in a new window

Periods of GIC being credited.
Opens in a new window

After the first update has been input, a message will be displayed 'Do you want the subsequent date to be reset?'

Select yes.

All dates up to and including yesterday's date will then be re-set. The red End date of the remission period will be removed and will need to be re-entered once all updates are completed. If the first update is before the red start date of the remission period, the start date is also removed and will need to be re-entered.




3

	

Once all updates are completed and the start date (where applicable) and end date of the remission period have been re-entered, delete all periods after the final remission by:

going to the row below the last red End date
deleting the start date in column B of the above row. This will automatically remove all periods after the final remission.

In column D, update the rows with the relevant Code up to including the last red End date.




4

	

In column L, select the GIC radio button.

The remission will appear in the Remission field in the top right hand corner of the spreadsheet.




5

	

Record the remission amount for this shortfall as shown in the Total remission field.

Input the Taxpayer name and Year(s)/Periods.

Save the GIC calculator output as a PDF to your local drive and attach to Siebel. Refer to Update activity - attach file, URL or document.
Opens in a new window




6

	

Check if there are more shortfall amounts for remission in the Debt Referral. If:

yes,

select the 'Clear All' button on the top of the spreadsheet to remove the previous data

repeat the process of calculating the GIC remission for each shortfall, return to step 2 of Calculate remission for a shortfall

In some instances it may be possible to include all debit amendments on one calculation sheet. This is typical when the remission period for each period starts on the due date of the activity statement and ends on the same day.

refer to Single calculation sheet
Opens in a new window
 for an example of how this is input

no, go to step 7.




7

	

Prior to inputting the total remission, compare your total GIC remission calculated figure to the total amended GIC (applied automatically to the account post the amendments).

If your calculated GIC remission figure is greater than the imposed amended GIC, you will need to review the account to determine why this occurred and adjust your calculations accordingly.

Failure to do so may lead to an overstated remission.

Due to the complexities of the ICP accounts it may be difficult to achieve a match between your manually calculated figure and the figures on the taxpayer's account in ICP. Any decision to proceed to remit the calculated figure (where there is a discrepancy) needs to be based on the risk.




8

	

Add up the total GIC remission as recorded in step 5.

Remit the total GIC as a single amount (that is, not one entry for each shortfall) from the account in ICP.

For more information, refer to Cancel or remit - remit GIC end to end process.
Opens in a new window

FC Firmer and Legal Recovery staff only: If the remit to base decision was made by a FC Firmer and Legal Recovery operative, request this operative to continue with Outcomes in GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation request.
Opens in a new window

This is the end of this procedure.

Remit SIC
Step	Action


1

	

Check whether the SIC to be remitted was imposed manually or automatically.

If SIC transaction was imposed:

automatically, select the Remit, adjust or Cancel Automated Penalty or Interest link at the page navigation bar. This will display relevant remission screen

manually, select the Remit, adjust or Cancel Manual Penalty, Cost or fine link at the page navigation bar. This will display relevant remission screen.




2

	

Type the amount of SIC to be remitted in the Remission Amount field.

The other remission types are not available for SIC and a dollar amount must be entered.


3	

Include the Siebel Activity ID: input the Activity ID from your Siebel activity, or if you are a case manager use the CATs activity ID from your case.

Note: When processing referrals from another business area you must include the Siebel Activity ID included in the referral.

For more information refer to CATs Enterprise Process.
Opens in a new window




4

	

Select the appropriate reason code for the remission from the Input Reason drop-down menu.

Select Save.




5

	

If SIC is being remitted on more than one period, repeat steps 1 to 4 in this table until all relevant periods have been actioned.

