FTL penalty guideline

Information on failure to lodge on time (FTL) penalties for Frontline Compliance and Debt Assist staff.

Last published 1 July 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page provides Frontline Compliance and Debt Assist staff with information on failure to lodge on time (FTL) penalties.

Definitions

For the purpose of this instruction, read the term:

'agent' as a registered tax agent and/or registered BAS agent

'taxpayer' as

a taxpayer

an agent, or

an authorised representative on behalf of the taxpayer.

Recent updates
01/07/2026	Update to 'Applying FTL penalty' section.	SD-105925


12/05/2026

	Update under 'Exclusions'.
	SD-104650
In Detail
FTL penalties

Taxpayers are liable to FTL penalty
Opens in a new window
 when they fail to lodge a document in the approved form by the due date.

The intention of applying FTL penalty is to encourage the lodgment of various documents by the due date and influence the behaviour of taxpayers who lodge late.

Liability to FTL penalty for failing to give the Commissioner an approved form on time is provided by subsection 286-75(1)
Opens in a new window
 of Schedule 1 to the Taxation Administration Act 1953 (TAA). The law provides for a liability of a base penalty amount (BPA) of one penalty unit for each period of 28 days overdue or part thereof, up to a maximum of five penalty units.

The penalty is applied at a higher rate for entities classified as medium (BPA times two) or large (BPA times five).

FTL penalty is applied, cancelled and remitted in accordance with PS LA 2011/19
Opens in a new window
 Administration of the penalty for failure to lodge on time.

In certain circumstances an exemption from FTL penalty may be granted to a taxpayer under safe harbour provisions.

Documents that FTL penalties can be raised

FTL penalty provisions have been in operation since 1 July 2000 and the penalty may be imposed at law for late or non-lodgment of the following forms:

activity statements (AS) including

quarterly activity statements

monthly activity statements

instalment notices including

quarterly instalment notices

annual instalment notices

annual GST returns (recorded on IPS as GSTR or GSTA) for

GSTR, the 2000/01 and later years

GSTA, the 2004/05 and later years

income tax (IT) returns for the 2000/01 year and later years

self-review assessment income tax exempt for the 2023-24 year and later years

pay as you go (PAYG) withholding annual reports for the 2000/01 and later years

fringe benefits tax (FBT) returns for the 2001/02 FBT year and later years

departing Australian superannuation payment (DASP) annual reports for the 2002/03 and later years

member contributions statements (MCS) for the 2007/08 year and later years for APRA regulated funds

lost member statements (LMS) for the period ended 30 June 2009 and later periods

taxable payments annual reports (TPAR)

from 1 July 2012 for businesses in the building and construction industry

from 1 July 2017 for some government related entities

from 1 July 2018 for businesses providing cleaning and courier services

from 1 July 2019 for businesses providing road freight, information technology services, security, investigation or surveillance services

any other document, statement, return, etc that is an approved form as defined by section 388-50
Opens in a new window
 of Schedule 1 to the TAA and is required to be given to the Commissioner by a particular day.

Operation of subsection 286-75(2)
Opens in a new window
 of Schedule 1 to the TAA specifically excludes from FTL penalty documents required under the following Acts:

Superannuation Contributions Tax (Assessment and Collection) Act 1997

Superannuation Guarantee (Administration) Act 1992, and

Superannuation (Self Managed Superannuation Funds) Supervisory Levy Imposition Act 1991.

Refer to exclusions for a list of FTL penalty exclusions that must be considered before any penalty application.

FTL penalty systems

FTL penalty is applied, remitted or cancelled via:

Integrated core processing (ICP) for obligations in ICP such as IT and FBT, or

Corporate penalty system
Opens in a new window
 (CPS) for other returns and AS.

Applied FTL penalty amounts will appear in ICP. Details of a specific FTL penalty can be viewed in the ICP:

transactions screen
Opens in a new window
 to view penalty for a specific period, or

statement of account screen
Opens in a new window
 to view an entity's penalty history.

The transactions screen will display a list of transactions for the period. FTL penalty application transactions will show as transaction type penalty, cost or fine. Any penalty remissions or cancellations will be displayed as separate transactions in this list.

This screen will display the transaction Id, the penalty amount, effective date and processing (input) date.

To view additional penalty details, select the transaction Id link for the relevant FTL penalty action. This will display details of the penalty, including:

the penalty type, including the entity size used in the calculation

original penalty amount

penalty units used in the calculation (if applicable)

input date, and

any remission amount.

The effective date can be accessed by selecting view transaction financials from the view manual, penalty, cost or fine screen.

FTL penalty and Safe harbour provisions

Under section 286-75
Opens in a new window
 of Schedule 1 to the TAA, penalty 'safe harbour' provisions apply to lodgment obligations with a due date on or after 1 March 2010. This includes instances where a deferral is granted for a document due before 1 March 2010 and the due date is now on or after 1 March 2010.

Information
alert:

Although lodgment of activity statements due on 28 February 2010 (a Sunday) were accepted by Tuesday 2 March 2010 without penalty (Monday 1 March 2010 being a public holiday in Perth), these statements are not covered by safe harbour.

Under the safe harbour provisions, taxpayers are not liable for FTL penalties raised due to late lodgment of a return, notice, statement or other document in the approved form where the document was lodged by an agent, and:

the taxpayer can prove they provided the agent with all relevant information required by the agent to lodge the document by the due date

the failure to lodge on time did not result from

intentional disregard by the agent of a taxation law

recklessness by the agent as to the operation of a taxation law.

The evidential burden as to when all relevant taxation information was given to the agent lies with the taxpayer. The taxpayer must be able to provide sufficient evidence of their claims.

To reduce administrative cost, requests for safe harbour exemption from FTL penalties will not be considered until the document has been lodged and an FTL penalty applied.

If safe harbour request is:

agreed to, the FTL penalty will be cancelled (not remitted)

not agreed with, the request will be considered for remission of FTL penalty.

Requests for safe harbour exemptions from FTL penalty can be made as detailed on Safe harbour.
Opens in a new window

FTL penalty and prosecution action

A business decision has been made to manually apply FTL penalty on unlodged documents before escalating them for prosecution action. The only exception to this is where a suitable exclusion category exists or where the penalty has been remitted/cancelled and cannot be reapplied. Where such an exception exists it should be recorded in accordance with the Recommend escalation for prosecution action decision in Recording actions.
Opens in a new window

Under section 8ZE
Opens in a new window
 of the TAA, there is no liability to FTL penalty if prosecution action is instituted.

This section does not give the Commissioner any discretion in relation to the FTL penalty when prosecution action is commenced. Any FTL penalty applied in regard to documents subject to prosecution action must be cancelled by the ATO prosecutions area at the time the summons/court attendance notice/complaint is issued.

It is the Commissioner's view that FTL penalty must be cancelled even if the entity prosecuted was not the same entity on which FTL penalty has been applied. That is, if we prosecute trustees on behalf of trusts, partners on behalf of partnerships, company directors on behalf of companies, etc, then the FTL penalty applied to the obligations of the trust, partnership or company must still be cancelled.

Under section 8ZE
Opens in a new window
 of the TAA, FTL penalty cannot be reapplied where prosecution action is subsequently withdrawn. This includes situations where a taxpayer has failed to lodge documents and prosecution action has commenced, that is a summons has issued to the taxpayer, FTL penalty cannot be applied/reapplied to the documents subject to the summons.

Exclusions
Legislative exclusions

Crown entities - Section 2B
Opens in a new window
 of the TAA prevents State and Territory Crown entities from being subject to administrative penalties such as FTL penalty. As a result, FTL penalty is not to be applied to Crown entities.

Crown entities will be identified by an accounting treatment.

Single touch payroll (STP) - With the introduction of STP the law excuses a taxpayer from reporting PAYG withholding (PAYG W) amounts in an AS when they have already reported these amounts through STP. Therefore, do not apply manual FTL penalty on an AS where:

the taxpayer has an STP reporting role that is active for any period of time within the PAYG W reporting period for that AS, and

the overdue AS is for

PAYG W only, or

PAYG W in conjunction with a PAYG instalment amount
Opens in a new window
 and/or GST instalment only.

For STP, where the taxpayer has a GST reporting method other than the GST instalment method, or reports PAYG instalments by the rate
Opens in a new window
 method, then FTL penalty may still be applied on that AS where PAYG W is also present.

System exclusions

The CPS and ICP systems support business rules designed to exclude specific entities and/or documents from the automatic application of FTL penalty.

Examples of entity types excluded are:

charities with an active income tax exempt charity (ITEC) role (IT returns only)

companies in liquidation or insolvent or bankrupt taxpayers

senior Australians - this exclusion is automatic in CPS only. For further information on age requirements refer to Eligibility for Age Pension
Opens in a new window

diplomats

deceased taxpayers

non-resident individual taxpayers

non-resident non-individual taxpayers (IT returns only) with the exception of LB&I non-individuals

partnerships (IT returns only), and

untraceable taxpayers or taxpayers with returned unclaimed mail indicators against addresses.

The exclusions in ICP are similar to those in CPS, however the following exclusions do not automatically apply in ICP and must be considered when making your decision to apply FTL penalty:

senior Australians, and

non-resident non-individual taxpayers (IT returns only).

Documents that result in a refund, nil or a small debit balance are also excluded from automatic FTL penalty application.

FTL penalty will not be applied if the entity, for the relevant role, has an:

unreliable address

incorrect name

invalid account type, or

invalid entity type.

These are exclusion categories detected by CPS which cannot be manually overridden. For these exclusions a pop-up message will appear in CPS and the penalty cannot be applied until these issues are rectified.

For obligations in ICP, incorrect/invalid errors must be rectified before an automatic FTL penalty can be applied.

The above exclusion rules also apply, with the exception of the refund, nil or small debit balance exclusion, to the manual application of FTL penalty.

In CPS you will be alerted that exclusions exist via the relevant check box and only users with the appropriate access can override.

In ICP there is nothing to alert users that exclusions exist. Therefore, you must ensure relevant checks for exclusion categories are undertaken before manually applying FTL penalty in ICP.

Business exclusions

PAYG instalment (PAYG I) only role exclusion

When considering applying FTL penalty on an overdue activity statement, the taxpayer will have a PAYG I only role exclusion if all of the following conditions are met:

entity is a PAYG I only remitter (entities whose only obligation for the relevant activity statement is PAYG I), and

the relevant activity statement does not relate to September 2000 and/or December 2000, and

entity's PAYG I reporting method on their activity statement is via the amount method (also called the GDP adjusted notional tax method). Activity statements using the amount method will have PAYG income tax instalment written at label 5A and an amount pre-printed at label T7. For further information refer to PAYG instalments.
Opens in a new window

Overriding systems exclusions

In CPS, the exclusion rules may be overridden where it is determined there is a significant revenue or compliance risk.

Consider the following factors when deciding to override an exclusion:

business, industry, sociological, economic and psychological (BISEP) factors (for further information refer to the compliance model
Opens in a new window
)

historical and current compliance behaviours

if a significant risk to revenue exists and lodgment of the overdue documents will result in significant debt

if large numbers of documents are overdue and repeated demands for lodgment have been continually ignored creating a significant compliance risk

non-lodgment pattern, and

payment pattern and whether it is regular or not.

A submission must be prepared and submitted to an authorised officer for an exclusion rule to be overridden. If the authorised officer approves the submission, they will apply the FTL penalty via the relevant system.

For further information refer to CPS - Exclusion
Opens in a new window
 or consult your support staff.

Applying FTL penalty

FTL penalties will be applied to unlodged documents either manually or by bulk processes. Manual FTL penalty application should be considered as a compliance tool when actioning a telephony lodgment campaign call, work management activity or Siebel case. When FTL penalty is to be manually processed, it will be applied in accordance with FTL penalty - Apply.
Opens in a new window

As part of your profiling
Opens in a new window
 you will still need to check if FTL penalties have previously been applied, and where applicable, consider applying on the documents you are actioning.

FTL penalty is applied/topped up based on:

the original/deferred lodgment due date

the date of the first day of each 28-day period or part thereof, to which the penalty applies

the value of the penalty unit

the entity size at the date the document was due.

The amount of FTL penalty to be applied is calculated by determining the BPA and whether the BPA is to be increased based on the size of the entity. The BPA will be one penalty unit for each period of 28 days or part thereof that a document was/is overdue, up to a maximum of five penalty units.

You must consider any lodgment concessions granted by the Commissioner before applying the penalty. This includes the concessional dates for agents, taxpayers living in areas affected by natural disasters, etc.

FTL penalty is calculated from the original or deferred lodgment due date. Do not add any additional days to the due date, for example if the due date falls on a weekend or a public holiday.

For documents that are:

1 to 28 days late, then one 28-day period will apply

29 to 56 days late, then two 28-day periods will apply

57 to 84 days late, then three 28-day periods will apply

85 to 112 days late, then four 28-day periods will apply

113 or more days late, then five 28-day periods will apply.

The value of the penalty unit to be used must be determined for each of the five 28-day periods. If the due date or date of the first day of the 28-day period is:

before 28 December 2012, use $110

from 28 December 2012 to 30 July 2015, use $170

from 31 July 2015 to 30 June 2017, use $180

from 1 July 2017 to 30 June 2020, use $210

from 1 July 2020 to 31 December 2022, use $222

from 1 January 2023 to 30 June 2023, use $275

from 1 July 2023 to 6 November 2024, use $313

from 7 November 2024 to 30 June 2026, use $330

from 1 July 2026, use $364.

Documents with a due date:

after 6 September 2012 and before 28 December 2012 will have 28-day periods subject to penalty unit amounts of $110 or $170

after 9 April 2015 and before 31 July 2015 will have 28-day periods subject to penalty unit amounts of $170 or $180

after 10 March 2017 and before 1 July 2017 will have 28-day periods subject to penalty unit amounts of $180 or $210

after 10 March 2020 and before 1 July 2020 will have 28-day periods subject to penalty unit amounts of $210 or $222

after 10 September 2022 and before 1 January 2023 will have 28-day periods subject to penalty unit amounts of $222 or $275

after 10 March 2023 and before 1 July 2023 will have 28-day periods subject to penalty unit amounts of $275 or $313

after 17 July 2024 and before 7 November 2024 will have 28-day periods subject to penalty unit amounts of $313 or $330

after 10 March 2026 and before 1 July 2026 will have 28-day periods subject to penalty unit amounts of $330 or $364.

The BPA will be increased depending on the entity size test:

if Small, BPA multiplied by one

if Medium BPA multiplied by two

if Large, BPA multiplied by five.

Where a document is overdue, the amount of manual FTL penalty to be applied will be calculated from the lodgment due date, to the day before the penalty is applied.

FTL penalty can be calculated using the following formula:

(BPA x number of periods overdue) X entity size

Example 1 - Small entity

A small entity has an overdue document due on 28 November 2012 and remains unlodged as at 2 April 2013 when manual FTL penalty is applied.

Based on these dates the document has been overdue for 125 days, therefore:

5 periods of 28 days or part thereof will apply

the first day of two of the periods of 28 days or part thereof occur before 28 December 2012

the first day of the remaining three periods of 28 days or part thereof occur after 28 December 2012 and before 31 July 2015.

As such penalty units of $110 and $170 will apply. The formula (BPA x number of periods overdue) X entity size will need to be applied to each BPA amount in order to reach the total FTL penalty to be applied.

The FTL penalty calculation would be:

[($110 x 2) x 1] + [($170 x 3) x 1] = $730.

This calculation can also be expressed in the following way:

