The relevant dates for determining the entity's size for the purposes of FTL penalty are:

the month when the document was required to be given, or

the income year in which the document is required to be given, or

the date in the month in which the document was required to be given.

Therefore, FTL penalty is applied/topped up as per the entity size at the date the document was due to be lodged. This would be the original/deferred lodgment due date as detailed on the relevant ATO system.

Topping up FTL penalty

Under subsection 286-80(6)
Opens in a new window
 of Schedule 1 to the TAA, it is recognised the original amount of FTL penalty imposed may not be final because the Commissioner may not have all the relevant information at that time.

FTL penalty is topped up in accordance with the entity size at the date the document was due to be lodged. This is important when topping up FTL penalty as the rate of FTL penalty originally applied may not reflect the actual size of the entity. This can arise because documents lodged after the initial FTL penalty was applied may determine the entity's actual size for FTL penalty purposes is different to that which was assumed when the penalty was initially applied.

If FTL penalty was originally applied at the incorrect size, then:

for CPS, the penalty must be cancelled and applied correctly

for ICP where the entity size has

increased, top up the penalty to the correct penalty dollar amount

decreased, the penalty must be cancelled and applied correctly. You must also notify the taxpayer that the original penalty has been cancelled and a new amount will be applied.

Late lodged documents

For late lodged documents, FTL penalty is automatically applied via batch processing in either ICP or CPS depending on the obligation.

In ICP, the following tests determine entity size for penalty purposes:

for income tax, assessable income from the late lodged document is used to calculate the penalty for that document

for any other document, the assessable income recorded in the income tax return that is equal to the income year of the

document being processed is used to determine entity size.

Where a document is lodged late, the amount of FTL penalty to be applied will be calculated from the day the document is due to the day before the document is received.

Where assessable income could not be identified for the same income year or no relevant income tax lodgment exists, then ICP will consider the entity to be small for penalty purposes.

CPS

Currently CPS allows FTL penalty to be applied manually on:

AS (monthly and quarterly)

annual GST returns (GSTR and GSTA)

pay as you go (PAYG) withholding where ABN not quoted annual reports, and

PAYG withholding annual reports (shown in CPS as payment summary annual report).

The calculation details are displayed on the 'failure to lodge penalty confirmation' window in CPS.

ICP

To apply FTL penalty the relevant account, role and period must be present. It is applied from the periods view for the relevant entity and role.

FTL penalty is applied in ICP by entering either the:

penalty dollar amount, or

number of penalty units.

Note: If entering a penalty dollar amount, the FTL penalty calculator
Opens in a new window
 should be used in accordance with FTL penalty apply.
Opens in a new window

FTL penalty warning

FTL penalty warning is not required prior to penalty imposition if the taxpayer is aware of their lodgment obligation.

For non-lodgment staff

Circumstances that may satisfy awareness of lodgment obligation can include:

previous lodgment of obligations
director/authorised representative have been previously made aware of FTL and lodgment obligations on any or all the entities they currently represent (including their own).

Officers will need to exercise greater judgment in determining whether the taxpayer is aware of their lodgment obligation and the consequences of non-lodgment, refer to Taxpayer profiling guideline.
Opens in a new window

The current business decision is not to apply FTL penalty where the FTL penalty warning or FTL penalty application occurred for a different type of obligation. For example, you cannot apply FTL penalty to an AS obligation if the FTL penalty warning was given for an IT obligation.

There is no requirement for FTL penalty warning or application of FTL penalty in the immediate prior period (IPP) as the IPP rule is not present in PS LA 2011/19
Opens in a new window
 - Administration of the penalty for failure to lodge on time.

Once FTL penalty has been applied, the taxpayer must receive written notification
Opens in a new window
 of its application. This is generally in the form of an auto-issued FTL penalty notice.

Consideration of whether to apply FTL penalty

Application of FTL penalty may be considered for all entity types including non-taxable entities.

In accordance with PS LA 2011/19
Opens in a new window
 - Administration of the penalty for failure to lodge on time it is essential officers take into account the individual circumstances of the taxpayer when considering whether or not to apply FTL penalty.

In deciding whether to apply FTL penalty it is therefore necessary to consider the following:

the compliance history of the taxpayer

the effort taken by the ATO to obtain lodgment

the value of the information required

whether the taxpayer has had an opportunity to comply

whether the taxpayer is aware of their lodgment obligation and that they may be liable for the penalty

any contact the taxpayer may have had with the ATO before the due date for lodgment

the length of time the document was overdue.

There may also be situations where a taxpayer has not lodged overdue documents due to circumstances beyond their control, such as illness or natural disasters. These extenuating circumstances may make it inappropriate to apply FTL penalty.

In the lead up to Christmas and ATO shutdown, manual FTL penalty should not be applied unless it is an extremely high risk case. Refer to the Non-lodgment actions overview
Opens in a new window
 for more details.

Where the above considerations have been satisfied, FTL penalty can be manually applied:

immediately after taxpayer contact, such as an outbound phone call, where you have determined the taxpayer is aware of FTL penalty implications

for IT and FBT, before, after or at the same time as the issue of a final notice.

Cancellation of FTL penalty

An FTL penalty should only be cancelled where it should not exist on the entity's account. If an FTL penalty has been applied correctly, in accordance with legislation, then any request to review the penalty must be considered as a remission not a cancellation.

Cancellation of the FTL penalty is only appropriate where:

FTL penalty was applied in error

FTL penalty was applied to an unlodged document, and it is subsequently found

the document was lodged but not recorded on our systems, or

there was no requirement to lodge, for example, the document has been made not necessary,
Opens in a new window
 or an activity statement has been terminated due to a backdated ABN/role cancellation

FTL penalty calculated at top up is less than the amount previously applied and penalty is to be cancelled and reapplied at the correct amount

the entity is insolvent and the penalty has been applied to the insolvent Integrated Client Account (ICA) account for a period after the entity became insolvent, or

safe harbour exemption from FTL penalty has been agreed to (refer such requests in accordance with the Referral instruction
Opens in a new window
).

For obligations other than IT, FTL penalty must be cancelled in the system in which the FTL penalty was applied. For IT, FTL penalty will be cancelled in ICP irrespective of the system in which the penalty was originally applied.

No specific authorisation level is required to cancel FTL penalty.

Where an FTL penalty notice has issued to a taxpayer and the FTL penalty is subsequently cancelled, the taxpayer must be notified of the cancellation. The notification is to advise the taxpayer a decision has been made to cancel the FTL penalty and to confirm corrective action has taken place. Notification can generally be provided verbally. It must be in writing where Prosecutions has cancelled the FTL penalty or the taxpayer has requested written confirmation.

For instructions on cancelling FTL penalty and further information on the appropriate letter to issue, refer to GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation.
Opens in a new window

Small business lodgment (FTL) amnesty program

The Small Business Lodgment (FTL) Amnesty announced in the Federal Budget for 2023 commenced on 1 June 2023.

To be eligible for the remission of their failure to lodge (FTL) penalty under the amnesty, the small business taxpayer must meet the conditions below:

their late obligations (for income tax, fringe benefit tax or business activity statements) were originally due to be lodged between 1 December 2019 and 28 February 2022

when the obligation was originally due to be lodged, the small business must have been an entity with an aggregated turnover less than $10 million

they are not a privately owned group or individual controlling over $5 million of net wealth

they must lodge their obligation during the amnesty period 1 June 2023 and 31 December 2023.

When speaking to a taxpayer regarding this amnesty advise:

they may still receive a FTL penalty for their late lodgment, even if they lodge during the amnesty period
where they are eligible for FTL penalty remission, we will process the remission within 21 days of the lodgment processing date and no action is required by the taxpayer
they can check their online account after 21 days to confirm the penalty has remitted.

If after 21 days their FTL penalty has not remitted:

they are not eligible for a remission under the amnesty, but
they may be eligible through normal processes, refer to GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation request.
Opens in a new window

For more information and scenarios, refer to Small business - Lodgment (FTL) amnesty program.
Opens in a new window

Remission of FTL penalty

The legislation supporting penalty remission is section 298-20
Opens in a new window
 of Schedule 1 to the TAA.

FTL remission request must be considered by an officer who did not make the decision to impose the FTL penalty.

If the penalty was imposed by:

a different operative, consider remission of the penalty

you, the remission request must be considered by another operative

For obligations other than IT, FTL penalty must be remitted in the system in which the FTL penalty was applied. For IT, FTL penalty will be remitted in ICP irrespective of the system in which the penalty was originally applied.

Principles for remission of FTL penalty

When considering an FTL penalty remission, the following principles apply:

FTL penalty remission requests can be made
verbally for request below $10,000, or
in writing - go to Remission of penalties
