 - Administration of the penalty for failure to lodge on time
the ATO Charter,
Opens in a new window
 and
the Compliance model principles
Opens in a new window
to decide if full remission can be granted, refer to FTL remission decision guidelines and tools.
Opens in a new window
Authorisation for FTL penalty remission

Subsection 298-20(1)
Opens in a new window
 of Schedule 1 to the TAA gives the Commissioner the power to remit FTL penalty. The Commissioner has delegated this power to the Deputy Commissioner, who in turn authorises certain tax officers to grant FTL penalty remissions.

Where you do not have the authority to grant an FTL penalty remission, you can make recommendations and seek approval from an officer who does have the appropriate authorisation.

Refer to section 1.16.4 of the Taxation authorisation guidelines
Opens in a new window
 to determine the amount of FTL penalty you are authorised to remit.

Communicating your decision to the taxpayer

When partial or no remission is granted, staff must issue the decision in writing to the taxpayer to enable review rights and include:

reasons for the decision
their review rights
contact details.

The legislative requirement to notify in writing of a penalty decision is set out in subsection 298-20(2)
Opens in a new window
 of Schedule 1 to the TAA.

When full remission is granted there is no legislative requirement to notify the taxpayer of the decision in writing. A decision on how the taxpayer is advised is to be made on a case by case basis, it is sufficient to phone the taxpayer and inform them remission has been granted in full.

However, if the taxpayer requests written confirmation, a letter advising remission has been granted in full must be sent.

Recording your actions

For instructions on either granting or denying a request for FTL penalty remission, including recording your actions, refer to GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation.
Opens in a new window

You must document the factors considered when granting or denying a request for remission of FTL penalty and the reasons for making the decision. This is important because:

it is consistent with legislation, procedures and expectations of tax officers, who are accountable for their actions and decisions

external organisations such as the Commonwealth Ombudsman, the Courts and the Administrative Review Tribunal may review ATO records

taxpayers may obtain copies of ATO records via the Freedom of Information Act 1982, or

taxpayers may seek statements of the reasons for decisions as part of Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977 (ADJR) review processes.

Review rights

Decisions on FTL penalty remission requests are reviewable both informally and formally. Formal review rights are outlined in the remission decision letter sent to the taxpayer.

The ATO's preferred method for reviewing adverse decisions is for the taxpayer to:

firstly, discuss the decision with the original decision maker, and

if still dissatisfied, discuss the decision with the officers team leader.

Formal review rights are available under Part IVC
Opens in a new window
 of the TAA where the Commissioner has refused to remit the penalty in full and the amount of penalty payable after the refusal is more than two penalty units.

Where a decision by the Commissioner not to remit a penalty cannot be reviewed under Part IVC of the TAA (that is, the amount of penalty that remains payable after the refusal to remit is two penalty units or less), the taxpayer may seek a review under the ADJR.

