Scenarios
Natural disasters

Many taxpayers have been affected by natural disasters including floods and bushfires. Be proactive in determining if a taxpayer has in any way been affected by these disasters.

As part of your natural conversations, discuss the potential impacts your taxpayers may be experiencing. This is applicable to all taxpayers not just those listed in the current impacted postcode lists. Employment, business supply chains, holidays and family members of taxpayers may be directly affected.

Go to Hardship - Disaster
Opens in a new window
 before continuing on this page.

Financial difficulties

An individual or business can be considered to have financial difficulties where unexpected or extenuating circumstances leave them unable to pay their debt for a short period of time.

For more information refer to Tax support for individuals, businesses, not-for-profits, and tax professionals
Opens in a new window
 on the ATO website.

Where the taxpayer has called because their bank account is frozen due to a fraudulent ATO refund, go to Refunds resulting in frozen bank accounts.
Opens in a new window

Where appropriate continue below.

Serious Hardship

An individual is in serious hardship when payment of a primary tax debt will result in them not having the means to pay for basic needs, such as:

food

clothing

medical supplies

accommodation, and

education for themselves, their family or dependants.

For more information, refer to Personal crisis or financial hardship
Opens in a new window
 on the ATO website.

Experience of vulnerability

The experience of vulnerability can impact a taxpayer in different ways. It can be temporary, long term or a permanent state. It is difficult to describe because of the complexity and personal circumstances a taxpayer may face.

We must seek to understand our taxpayer through the factors or barriers stopping them from paying, lodging and engaging. These factors may include, but are not limited to:

life events (illness, relationship breakdown, domestic violence, bereavement)

access barriers (remoteness, cultural and linguistic diversity, incarceration)

economic barriers (low financial literacy and knowledge, homelessness, financial coercion)

psychological barriers (mental health issues, fear).

Experiencing these factors does not automatically indicate that someone cannot engage, lodge or pay. Majority of taxpayers do prioritise taxation obligations.

Taxpayers experiencing these barriers deserve our empathy and may need additional support. For more information, refer to Tailored support for taxpayers experiencing vulnerability.
Opens in a new window

Continue below.

In Detail
Supporting taxpayer's wellbeing

There are a range of factors that can affect a taxpayer's ability to meet their tax and super commitments. These include:

drought or a natural disaster

family tragedy

serious illness, and

financial misfortune.

A taxpayer's mental health and wellbeing should always come first.

Warning

If the taxpayer is in immediate danger, advise them to call 000 immediately.

Taxpayers can visit Financial difficulties, disasters and vulnerability support
Opens in a new window
 on the ATO website, to explore available support options.

If a taxpayer would like to access immediate mental health and wellbeing support they can contact:

Beyond Blue on 1300 224 636
Lifeline on 13 11 14.

These services provide confidential, free support and are available 24 hours a day.

Depending on their circumstances, there are a range of support options available to help taxpayers experiencing financial difficulties and hardship. Some options have strict legislative or policy requirements and may not be available in all circumstances. Refer to immediate assistance available.

Immediate assistance available

A range of support options are available. Not all options apply in every situation, and some have strict legislative or policy requirements.

Use the table below to support a clear and transparent discussion with the taxpayer by:

explaining relevant support options and key eligibility requirements
discussing which options may apply to the taxpayer’s circumstances and why
clearly explaining when an option is not available and the reasons for this, and
identifying next steps, including referrals or warm transfers where appropriate.

Where possible, support the taxpayer to get back on track or resolve their situation by considering options relevant to their circumstances.

ATO assistance
Assistance	Details
Payment plans	

A taxpayer experiencing financial difficulties or hardship may need a tailored payment plan that suits their circumstances.

When establishing a payment plan, all debts (including debts not yet due but visible on the account) must be included into payment plans. It will cause any active plan to default if a debt on the account is not included in a payment plan.

Payment plans should aim to finalise the debt in the shortest reasonable timeframe, noting that general interest charge (GIC) continues to accrue while amounts remain unpaid.

Go to Negotiating Payment Options.
