Scenarios

The taxpayer may be able to defer a compulsory repayment or overseas levy if they are experiencing serious hardship or fall into the 'other special reasons' category.

Serious hardship

Serious hardship exists where the taxpayer is unable to provide food, accommodation, clothing, medical treatment, education or other necessities for themselves, their family or other people for whom they are responsible.

Other special reasons

Other special reasons covers any unusual or exceptional circumstances that make it fair and reasonable for the taxpayer not to make their compulsory repayment. Other special reasons, for example include:

natural disasters affecting you
death or serious illness
other serious or difficult personal circumstances such as financial abuse, family or domestic violence.

If you believe the taxpayer may be experiencing vulnerability refer to Tailored support for taxpayers experiencing vulnerability.
Opens in a new window

Taxpayer wants to email the deferment form

Information

If a taxpayer is emailing evidence, advise that our in-box will not receive documents larger than 25 MB. Ensure their documents are smaller than that size.

The deferment is only for the year to which it applies. It is not a waiver of debt. If a deferment application is successful, the taxpayer does not have to make a repayment towards their debt for that particular income year. The amount deferred will remain part of the taxpayer's accumulated debt and continue to be indexed.

To defer the compulsory repayment or overseas levy the taxpayer should complete the Defer or amend your compulsory repayment or overseas levy (NAT 2471)
