Third-Party Intermediary (TPI) Fee

Taxpayers cannot access their superannuation to pay for TPI services. This is where they have assisted the taxpayer with applying for their application.

If the taxpayer has paid a fee to a TPI who is a recognised tax adviser and helped with their application, they can't claim this fee as a deduction in their income tax return.

However, if part of the fee includes advice about managing their tax affairs, they may be able to claim a deduction for that portion. See Cost of managing tax affairs.
Opens in a new window

Refund of Tax withheld from CRS release

The member may be eligible to receive a refund of the amount withheld where a Superfund:

has paid a super lump sum to a member under another condition of release
have withheld an amount and the member now provides medical certification that confirms they have a terminal medical condition.

They should provide the Superfund with the required medical certification stating that at the time of the payment, or within 90 days of receiving the payment, they had a terminal medical condition.

To refund the amount withheld, the Superfund needs to:

provide the member with a tax-free payment of the amount that was withheld from the original super lump sum
adjust the amount withheld downwards in their next activity statement payment period by the amount repaid.

If the member is applying after 21 July of the financial year following the payment, advise them to apply directly to us for a refund. They will need to provide a copy of their payment summary (if you provided one to them), and evidence of their medical certification.

A request for a refund can be sent to us by:

fax: 1300 669 846
mail
AUSTRALIAN TAXATION OFFICE
PO BOX 3100
PENRITH NSW
Aboriginal Community Benefit Fund (Youpla)

The Aboriginal Community Benefit Fund (Youpla) has collapsed.

This fund was set up as a funeral fund for the indigenous community. The fund allowed indigenous individuals to pay into the fund to cover any funeral expenses upon the death of the member.

Members are not receiving:

a benefit from the fund, or
a refund of money paid.

This may increase the number of funeral expense applications we receive for the indigenous community.

When assessing these applications, the taxpayer needs to meet the eligibility criteria as per legislation.

If you are speaking to a taxpayer, be aware of the sensitive nature of the situation.

Intelligence from a Third party

There may be instances where a third party (including medical practitioners and intermediaries) will report intelligence regarding an individual in relation to Compassionate release.

Intelligence from third parties will usually relate to a behaviour where an individual has:

not paid for the relevant expense after being approved for Compassionate release

applied for Compassionate release and was not eligible, or

possibly abused the Compassionate release program.

When intelligence is reported from a third party (via any inbound channel) that was not instigated by the ATO, staff need to:

record the details of the conversation in a new orphan activity (remove taxpayer name details in Siebel if Tip-Off has been received via inbound correspondence and already associated to a taxpayer)

complete the ATO Tip-Off
Opens in a new window
 form. Ensure to record the following details

user ID

name

the priority of the Tip-Off (do not discuss this with the informant). The form has guiding information to assist in determining the priority

activity ID for the orphan activity, and

details of the intelligence provided.

Members of Exempt Public Service Superannuation Schemes (EPSSS)

EPSSS are Commonwealth Government Non-regulated Super funds and are subject to state/territory laws on early release of super.

EPSSS entities:

Judges pension schemes (all states except WA)

Governors pension schemes (irrespective of state)

SA police superannuation scheme

WA parliamentary pension scheme

Papua New Guinea scheme

Government employees superannuation board.

If the taxpayer is not sure if their fund is an EPSSS, they can check Super fund lookup.

In most cases, members of these schemes need to apply directly with them unless they have directed the member to the ATO.

What is CRS

Advise the taxpayer:

super may be withdrawn under compassionate grounds for unpaid expenses in very limited circumstances
an expense is still classified as unpaid even if they have borrowed money to pay for the expense, e.g. a loan, credit card or from a family or friend
the amount of super they can withdraw is limited to what they reasonably need to meet the unpaid expense or the borrowed amount that is still outstanding
each application is individually assessed against the legislative requirements
we cannot guarantee the application will be approved
super should only be accessed when the taxpayer does not have the financial capacity to meet the expenses

we recommend they go to Access on compassionate grounds - what you need to know
