Scenarios
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islanders

If the taxpayer is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander and needs assistance, they can call the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Resource Centre on 13 10 30.

The centre can assist Indigenous taxpayers with a range of taxation matters.

Deceased

For more information, refer to Deceased person - Lodgment requirements.
Opens in a new window

Foreign residents

If the taxpayer is a foreign resident, then tax is generally withheld in Australia when they earn any:

interest

unfranked dividends, or

royalties.

Foreign residents do not need to lodge an Australian income tax return, if the:

above is the only Australian-sourced income they earnt, and

correct foreign resident withholding tax has been withheld.

Foreign residents have additional requirements to lodge, if they have a:

Higher Education Loan Program (HELP)

Australian Apprenticeship Support Loan (TSL), or

Vet Student Loan (VSL).

For more information on these requirements, refer to Study and training loans.
Opens in a new window

Income under threshold

If the taxpayer advises they are not required to lodge as they are under a certain threshold, advise:

there are several categories in the tax return instructions that may need lodgment of a tax return

although income may be under the threshold for one category, a return may still be required based on one of the other categories

to review the tax return instructions to make an accurate determination, before a non-lodgment advice can be considered.

No longer required to lodge (individual)

The taxpayer's account can be updated to record a FRNN, if they:

have not lodged a return for a few years, and

do not believe they will need to lodge a return in the future based on their current circumstances.

This means they will not need to ring the ATO each year to advise they are not required to lodge. If they later review they are required to lodge, they can lodge a tax return as normal.

To input an FRNN, the taxpayer will first need to review if they are not required to lodge for the relevant year.

Not-for-profit organisations

A not-for-profit (NFP) organisation might be eligible for income tax exemption.

For more information on specific eligibility criteria, refer taxpayers to Income tax exempt organisations.
Opens in a new window

Where a taxpayer needs assistance to check their eligibility for income tax exemption, transfer them to the NFP centre on 1300 130 248.

Note: A RNN cannot be entered for a NFP Self-review return.

Where a NFP is taxable (is ineligible to self-assess as income tax exempt), a RNN can be entered for the income tax return if:

the NFP is a NFP company, and

they advise their taxable income does not exceed $416.
Opens in a new window

A taxable NFP organisation needs to lodge an income tax return if the organisation is a:

NFP company and has taxable income greater than $416

taxable trust or partnership. They must lodge a return every year regardless of net income.

For more information, refer to Taxable NFP organisations.
Opens in a new window

Pre and post insolvency

Where a taxpayer becomes insolvent, they may lodge separate returns for the pre and post insolvency periods, or lodge a single return for the year.

If the taxpayer advises they have already lodged a return for the year of insolvency:

check for an Insolvency accounting treatment

review the Client calendar and confirm a return has been received on either the pre or post-insolvency account.

