GIC remission decision guidelines and tools

D-1 - Information to assess remission requests for general interest charges (GIC).

Last published 19 September 2024

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

D-1

Information to assist with the decision-making process when actioning a remission request for general interest charges (GIC).

The purpose of GIC is to:

encourage the timely payment of tax
deny late payers an unfair financial advantage over those who pay on time, and
compensate the Australian Government and the community for the impact of late payments.

The desired outcome is to achieve a decision that is consistent with both the law and Practice Statement Law Administration PS LA 2011/12
Opens in a new window
 - Remission of General Interest Charge.

Clients will need to satisfy us that remission is warranted, considering the nature of the specific event.

The exercise of power to remit must also:

be fair to the whole community
address unfair financial advantage
uphold community expectations.
Recent updates
21/08/2024	Added 'Requests involving GST Property Credits' section.	SD-82518
In Detail
GIC remission in specific situations

There are specific policies governing how GIC is applied in the following situations:

Disputed debts and 50/50 arrangements
Opens in a new window
Bankruptcy/Liquidation
Opens in a new window
Super Guarantee Charge
Excess contributions and Div 293 tax
Opens in a new window
Deceased estates
Opens in a new window
Mutual Agreement Procedures
Opens in a new window
Foreign Revenue claims.
Opens in a new window

For further information to confirm eligibility for remission for these specific situations, refer to PS LA 2011/12.
Opens in a new window

Requests involving GST Property Credits

The credit on the supplier's GST property credits account is transferred into the supplier's activity statement account on the date the relevant activity statement is lodged.

The effective date of the GST property credit transfer posting is the date the activity statement is lodged. So, if the activity statement is lodged after the due date, the GIC becomes payable.

When considering a GIC remission request:

only consider the client's reason for not lodging the relevant activity statement on time
the fact the GST property credit was paid into the GST property credits account at the time of the sale is not grounds for a remission.

For more information, go to GST property credit enquiries
Opens in a new window
 and GST at settlement.
Opens in a new window
 

Debt referred to external collection agency (ECA)

Do not update or make changes to the ECA case summary Id or referral activity in Siebel. Doing so will withdraw the client from the ECA.

Where you identify that a client has been referred to an ECA,
Opens in a new window
 remission requests can still be considered by the ATO, including request made via:

telephony
correspondence, and
online. 

Before actioning any GIC remission requests, follow the checks outlined in GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation request procedure.
Opens in a new window
 Complete before continuing to action the request. 

Superannuation guarantee charge (SGC) request

Only SG Shortfall GIC and SG Part 7 GIC can be considered for remission. The Commissioner does not have discretion to remit:

Nominal interest
Administration fee (excluding SG Amnesty)
SG Shortfall.

If the client is requesting a remission on SG Part 7 penalties, an objection
Opens in a new window
 must be lodged.

When considering remission of GIC on the SG Shortfall, it is important to note that SGC is:

payable by employers who failed to provide prescribed minimum level of superannuation support
an amount collected on behalf of employees and compensates employees for lost earnings.

Given the above, it may often be difficult to regard GIC remission for SGC as 'fair and reasonable'. However, each case must be examined on its merits taking into account particular facts.

A remission of GIC for late payment of SGC may be granted if there are special circumstances and because of those circumstances it would be fair and reasonable to remit all or a part of the charge.

A decision may be made to allow the remission, if all the following special circumstances arise. The:

debt on the SGC account is less than $50 and relates to GIC, and
remission would return the account to a zero balance.

This only applies to specific circumstances involving GIC on SGC accounts.

In addition to the above factors, the particular circumstances of each case should also be considered in determining whether remission is fair and reasonable.

The reasons and grounds for the decision must be clearly noted.

Telephony requests for GIC remission

You must complete the Telephony GIC remission application form
Opens in a new window
 and attach this to the Siebel activity for all telephony GIC remissions, excluding:

low value remissions
written requests where an outbound call is made for additional information (optional).

Open and save a copy of the Telephony GIC remission application form
Opens in a new window
 to your H:\ drive.

Grounds for remission

Information alert:
Full or partial payment of outstanding primary tax is not mandatory for consideration of GIC remission request.

GIC may be remitted on various grounds prescribed under:

the legislation Section 8AAG
Opens in a new window
 of the Tax Administration Act (TAA), and
in PS LA 2011/12.
Opens in a new window

These are outlined in the tasks below.

Task 1 - Circumstances which directly impacted the ability to pay by the due date
Step	Action


1

	

What directly caused the inability to pay the original debt by the due date?

Information
alert:
The ATO expects clients to organise their affairs to pay their tax debts on time.




2

	

How did this impact their ability to pay?

For example:

Was their income reduced and how?
Were there increased expenses such as they had to pay for urgent essential repairs or replace essential items damaged in an event?
Were funds or bank accounts frozen due to court proceedings?
Were funds stolen due to fraud or a scam?



3

	

What dates/timeframes are involved?

For example:

When did the circumstances occur in relation to the due date and are they still current now?
Does the requested GIC relate to the period where the circumstances occurred?
If the dates/timeframes only relate to part of the GIC amount requested, a partial remission may be considered.



4

	

Is there sufficient information to make a decision? If:

yes, go to step 5
no, go to GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation request.
Opens in a new window
 Under Outcomes select Sufficient information not provided.



5

	

Has the client demonstrated the direct link between the circumstance and the delay in payment? If:

yes, go to task 2
no,
the remission should be refused
go to Making a decision.
Task 2 - Steps taken to lessen the severity and impact of the circumstances
Step	Action


1

	

What steps were taken to lessen the severity and impact of the circumstances?

For example:

Have they sold non-essential assets, such as shares, or do they have non-essential assets that could have been sold to raise money to pay tax?
Did they seek finance?
Have they taken up opportunities to increase their income?
Did they defer or reschedule other financial commitments?
Were the steps taken within a reasonable timeframe relevant to the payment due date?
If it was not possible to take mitigating steps, why?

Note: There may be circumstances, such as some natural disasters, where it is not possible to take mitigating steps. This must be considered when making your decision.




2

	

Is there sufficient information to make a decision? If:

yes, go to step 3
no, go to Sufficient information not provided.
Opens in a new window



3

	

Has the client demonstrated they took reasonable steps or attempted to take steps to mitigate the circumstances? If:

yes, go to task 3
no,
the remission should be refused
go to Making a decision.
Task 3 - Confirm if the circumstances were beyond or within their control

We need to separate circumstances by determining if the circumstance/s were beyond or within the client's control.

 If...	Then...


beyond the client's control

	

we may remit where we are satisfied that the circumstances contributing to the delayed payment were not the client's fault.

Examples of circumstances beyond the client's control include but are not limited to:

natural disasters (such as fire, flood or drought)
industrial action
an unforeseen collapse of a major debtor of the client
the sudden ill health of the client, someone they care for, or key personnel in sole trader or small business situations.

Examples of circumstances beyond the client's control which would not usually justify a remission include:

general economic downturn, such as the cost-of-living crisis or inflation
a decline in business which impacts an entire industry (many other businesses in that industry are also experiencing these conditions but have taken steps to pay their taxes on time)
fluctuation of currency exchange rates.



within the client's control

	

if the delayed payment was caused by the client, we must also consider whether it is 'Fair and reasonable' to remit.

Examples of circumstances caused by the client can include but are not limited to:

business restructure
using funds set aside for tax to buy non-essential assets or pay other creditors in preference to paying ATO debts.

If the circumstances which caused the delay in payment were:

beyond the client's control, the remission should be granted. Go to Making a decision
within the client's control, go to task 4.
Task 4 - If within the client's control, is it fair and reasonable?

Information
alert:
Not required to be considered where circumstances are beyond the client's control.

Where circumstances are within the client's control, the decision to remit must be fair and reasonable.

Considerations:

A client who pays late should not be given any unfair financial advantage over clients who organise their affairs to ensure they can pay on time.
Would ordinary and reasonable members of the community who pay their taxes on time see the circumstances as fair and reasonable to remit?
Is it fair and reasonable to shift the cost of the late payment from the client to the community?

The below lists are not exhaustive.

The community may view it as fair and reasonable where a client:

has not received an unfair financial advantage
made a reasonable decision which results in unforeseen severe consequences affecting their ability to pay.

The community would not generally view it as fair and reasonable if late payment was due to:

a decline in business which impacts an entire industry. This is because many other businesses in that industry are also experiencing these conditions but have taken steps to pay their taxes on time
not prioritising tax liabilities. For example, client was too busy or on holiday.

Is it fair and reasonable to remit? If:

yes, the remission should be granted
no, the remission should be refused.

Go to Making a decision.

Making a decision

Considering the client's circumstances, relevant practice statement, resources, and desired outcome, decide:

whether the remission should be granted or refused, and
if granted, whether it should be a full or partial remission. Partial remission should be considered when the circumstances do not warrant a full remission
whether the remission of GIC is associated with income tax return amendments which included capital gain tax event details after property settlement has occurred. Refer to Taxable Determination 94/89.
Opens in a new window
Partial remission of GIC

A partial remission on GIC can occur when the circumstances do not warrant a full remission of the dollar amount or timeframe requested.

Partial remission example

A client requests remission of all GIC on the Integrated client account. A debtor went into liquidation 7 months ago resulting in the client's inability to pay. The officer decides to only remit GIC applied in the last 7 months. The GIC imposed earlier is not remitted as the client has not provided any circumstances for why the debt could not be paid.

Full remission example

A client has requested a remission of the GIC on their Income Tax account for the time they were unable to work due to having a broken leg. The officer decides to remit the GIC applied for the period requested by the client. Although the client has more GIC available, this is considered a full remission as the officer remitted the full amount of GIC for the timeframe requested by the client.

Special circumstances

What constitutes 'special circumstances' will depend on the facts in the particular case. You must consider each on its own merits.

Special circumstances can only be considered in extreme circumstances. For example:

hardship
Opens in a new window
 (bankruptcy vs remission of GIC)
vulnerable clients
Opens in a new window
 (e.g. domestic violence)
genuine mistake in completing their tax return (e.g. first time lodging a return).

To confirm if special circumstances apply, contact your support team.

Narrating your decision

When granting or refusing a remission, your note should explain how you made the decision referencing the prescribed grounds in PS LA 2011/12.
Opens in a new window

Your note must explain how you have arrived at your decision with consideration to the following:

Are circumstances beyond or within the client's control?
Did circumstances directly cause delayed payment?
Did the client take reasonable steps to mitigate the effects of those circumstances?
Where circumstances are within the client's control, is it fair and reasonable to remit and why? For example, it's not fair and reasonable to remit because <______>.

For partial remission, your note must also include:

why the circumstances do not warrant a full remission of the dollar amount or timeframe requested, and
how the approved amount/period was calculated.
Penalty Remission-Cancellation macro

This macro:

gathers interest details for a client including
ICP, and
previous remissions in last 5 years
allows an officer to indicate which entries they want to remit
may not gather GIC imposed over 5 years.

When undertaking a remission, the macro
Opens in a new window
 will check:

APS level and authorisations
amount of debt and payment arrangement status, and
overdue lodgments.
