A taxpayer who pays late should not be given any unfair financial advantage over taxpayers who organise their affairs to ensure they can pay on time.
Would ordinary and reasonable members of the community who pay their taxes on time see the circumstances as fair and reasonable to remit?
Is it fair and reasonable to shift the cost of the late payment from the taxpayer to the community?

The below lists are not exhaustive.

The community may view it as fair and reasonable where a taxpayer:

has not received an unfair financial advantage
made a reasonable decision which results in unforeseen severe consequences affecting their ability to pay.

The community would not generally view it as fair and reasonable if late payment was due to:

a decline in business which impacts an entire industry. This is because many other businesses in that industry are also experiencing these conditions but have taken steps to pay their taxes on time
not prioritising tax liabilities. For example, taxpayer was too busy or on holiday.
Making a decision

Considering the taxpayer's circumstances, relevant practice statement, resources, and desired outcome, decide:

whether the remission should be granted or refused, and
if granted, whether it should be a full or partial remission. Partial remission should be considered when the circumstances do not warrant a full remission.
Partial remission of GIC

A partial remission on GIC can occur when the circumstances do not warrant a full remission of the dollar amount or timeframe requested.

Partial remission example

A taxpayer requests remission of all GIC on the Integrated Client Account (ICA).

A debtor went into liquidation 7 months ago resulting in the taxpayer's inability to pay.

The officer decides to only remit GIC applied in the last 7 months. The GIC imposed earlier is not remitted as the taxpayer has not provided any circumstances for why the debt could not be paid.

Full remission example

A taxpayer has requested a remission of the GIC on their Income Tax account for the time they were unable to work due to having a broken leg.

The officer decides to remit the GIC applied for the period requested by the taxpayer.

Although the taxpayer has more GIC available, this is considered a full remission as the officer remitted the full amount of GIC for the timeframe requested by the taxpayer.

Special circumstances

What constitutes 'special circumstances' will depend on the facts in the particular case. You must consider each on its own merits.

Special circumstances can only be considered in extreme circumstances. For example:

hardship
