SG Shortfall.

If the client is requesting a remission on SG Part 7 penalties, an objection
Opens in a new window
 must be lodged.

When considering remission of GIC on the SG Shortfall, it is important to note that SGC is:

payable by employers who failed to provide prescribed minimum level of superannuation support
an amount collected on behalf of employees and compensates employees for lost earnings.

Given the above, it may often be difficult to regard GIC remission for SGC as 'fair and reasonable'. However, each case must be examined on its merits taking into account particular facts.

A remission of GIC for late payment of SGC may be granted if there are special circumstances and because of those circumstances it would be fair and reasonable to remit all or a part of the charge.

A decision may be made to allow the remission, if all the following special circumstances arise. The:

debt on the SGC account is less than $50 and relates to GIC, and
remission would return the account to a zero balance.

This only applies to specific circumstances involving GIC on SGC accounts.

