Information alert:
Use our self-service any time - it's easy, free and fast. Refer to Self-help services.
Opens in a new window

In Detail
Priority processing

Priority processing is only considered where the ATO holds USM and receives a successful claim.

A taxpayer can receive unclaimed super as a direct payment if the:

claimant is 

65 years of age or older 
a former temporary resident (previously held a temporary visa) 
a non-member spouse
diagnosed with a terminal medical condition
super was transferred to us as a small or insoluble lost member and the current balance is less than $200.

USM must be transferred into a complying super fund if above conditions are not met.

USM transferred or rolled over to a super fund will be subject to the release criteria for the super fund. For more information, go to How to claim unclaimed super.
Opens in a new window

Check to see if a USM claim has been received. If there is inbound correspondence, proceed with the request.

Discuss the hardship criteria and need for evidence of hardship with the taxpayer.

If the taxpayer is experiencing serious hardship:

the ATO may be able to action the taxpayer's refund or rollover as a priority
they will need to provide evidence of hardship.

Taxpayers are not eligible for a priority refund if they are:

not experiencing serious hardship, or 
unable to provide evidence of their serious hardship.
Hardship criteria

Serious hardship exists when the taxpayer is unable to provide and/or is experiencing:

accommodation. Examples include 
eviction
disconnection of essential services
repossession of house
food
clothing
medical treatment
education. For example, non-payment of school fees
other basic necessities for themselves, their family or other people they are responsible for. Examples include
funeral expenses
bail purposes
natural disaster situations
repossession of vehicle
family tragedy
serious illness.

The limitation of social activities, entertainment or the loss of access to goods or services of a more luxurious nature are not considered as hardship.

Evidence of hardship

Evidence of hardship includes:

official eviction notice (not a warning of possible eviction due to rental arrears)
pending disconnection of essential services, like water, electricity, gas, mobile phone or internet
notice of impending legal action
letter from charitable organisation regarding loss of employment and/or inability to provide for basic necessities
bank notice. For example, overdraft call or mortgaged property repossession. Bank statements or ATM receipts are not accepted as evidence
overdue medical bills
letter from doctor verifying the inability to earn an income due to illness or caring for a sick family member
final notice from school regarding payment of mandatory fees
funeral expenses, or
repossession notice of essential items. For example, car or motorcycle.

Advise taxpayers the timeframe for completion of a hardship claim is 7 to 10 business days once documentation is received.

Genuine serious hardship

If the taxpayer is experiencing genuine serious hardship and is able to provide evidence, advise them to fax this evidence to the ATO on 1300 138 757 within 48 hours.

Advise the taxpayer:

the receipt ID of the Siebel activity
to include on their evidence of hardship
their TFN
the words 'financial hardship'
their contact telephone number
the receipt ID for this call
the request will be actioned within 5 working days of receipt of the evidence. If we require more information, they will be contacted within 5 working days.
Timeframe exceeded

Check for:

any applicable notes on the taxpayer's file, and
receipt of evidence.

If hardship claim is outside of service standard, escalate to Superannuation unclaimed monies as follows:

