S-5, S-2 - Information on when benefits may be cashed out and the conditions of access due to severe financial hardship.

Last published 25 November 2024

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

S-5, S-2

This page provides guidelines on when benefits may be cashed out and when a member has or has not met preservation age + 39 weeks.

Recent updates
09/08/2023
	Change made in 'Access due to severe financial hardship'.	SD-72522, 69003
Self Help
ATO website
Success
alert:
Help the caller go to the page while on the phone.

This is a chance to educate the caller about self-help options, which may lead to:

the taxpayer using self-service in the future, and
a better taxpayer experience.

Taxpayers can obtain more information from the ATO website on:

Withdrawing and using your super
Opens in a new window

Early access to your super.
Opens in a new window

Information
alert:
Use our self-service any time - it's easy, free and fast. Refer to Self-help services.
Opens in a new window

In Detail
Summary

Accessing super due to severe financial hardship is granted by taxpayer's super fund. The ATO does not administer this function.

Where the member meet conditions of release for financial hardship, as determined by their fund, benefits may be cashed out.

The tax rates applied for these benefits are the same as most super lump sum payments.

If the taxpayer requires information on:

how much tax will be withheld, go to Super lump sums, tax offset, low rate cap and taxation
Opens in a new window

preservation age, go to Conditions of release overview.
Opens in a new window
Impacted by bushfire

Advise taxpayers impacted by the bushfires about other forms of support the ATO can offer: 

granting extensions for lodgment/payment
remitting penalties or interest
release from payment in certain circumstances, and
checking for unclaimed or lost super.

Refer to Hardship - Disaster
Opens in a new window
 and Bushfires
Opens in a new window
 for other avenues of assistance.

Services Australia is providing immediate support for people directly affected by bushfires in disaster declared areas.

Access due to severe financial hardship

Taxpayers may be able to withdraw some of their super if they are experiencing severe financial hardship. Access on grounds of severe financial hardship is not administered by the ATO, therefore taxpayers need to contact their super provider to request access under this ground. 

There are no special tax rates for a super withdrawal because of severe financial hardship. Withdrawals are paid and taxed as a normal super lump sum.

If you are: 

under 60 years old, this is generally taxed at between 17% and 22%
60 years old, you will not be taxed unless the lump sum includes an untaxed element.
Eligibility

Eligibility for access due to severe financial hardship depends on a taxpayers age in relation to their preservation age. For example, if their preservation age is 55 and they are:

under 55 years and 39 weeks old, they need to satisfy the conditions under option 1 below, or
over 55 years and 39 weeks old, they need to satisfy the conditions under option 2 below.
Option 1 - Under preservation age plus 39 weeks

If they are under their preservation age plus 39 weeks, they need to meet both these conditions:

they have received eligible government income support payments for a continuous period of 26 weeks, and
they are not able to meet reasonable and immediate family living expenses.

The minimum amount that can be withdrawn is $1,000 and the maximum is $10,000. If their super balance is less than $1,000, they can withdraw up to their remaining balance after tax.

They can only make one withdrawal in any 12-month period.

Option 2 - Reached preservation age plus 39 weeks

If they have reached their preservation age plus 39 weeks, they need to meet both these conditions:

they have received eligible government income support payments for a cumulative period of 39 weeks after they reached their preservation age, and
they were not gainfully employed at the time of applying.

There are no restrictions on how much they can withdraw if they meet the age and the other 2 conditions.

How to apply for access due to financial hardship

Taxpayers need to apply to their super fund directly for release of super on financial hardship grounds. The ATO does not process severe financial hardship requests.

If their super provider requests evidence, they can ask Services Australia to provide a letter confirming they have received eligible government income support payments for the relevant period.

For more information on how to apply for early access to their super because of financial hardship, see If you need to apply because of financial hardship at Services Australia.
