This page provides information about eligibility requirements for a priority refund request.

Last published 17 June 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page provides information about eligibility requirements for a priority refund request.

Recent updates

27/02/2026

	

Updated In Detail.

	

SD-102299

In Detail
Definition of serious hardship

A taxpayer is considered to be in serious hardship when they are left unable to provide immediate needs for:

themselves
their family, or
other people they are responsible for.

Serious hardship can also apply to businesses who require their refund to continue their business. For example, pay staff wages, purchase essential supplies, etc.

Factors contributing to serious hardship generally include:

family tragedy
financial misfortune
impacts of natural disaster, and
other serious or difficult circumstances. For example, serious illness may be taken into consideration.
Individual taxpayers

Serious hardship exists when you are unable to provide:

accommodation (for example, eviction, disconnection of essential services, repossession of house)
food
clothing
medical treatment
education (for example, non-payment of school fees), or
other basic necessities for

yourself
your family, or
other people you are responsible for (for example, funeral expenses, bail purposes, natural disaster situations, repossession of vehicle).

The financial need must be serious and immediate.

Business taxpayers

Serious hardship exists when the financial difficulty is associated with:

serious financial hardship (for example, the business will be closed)
disconnection of an essential service
repossession of a business vehicle or equipment required for business
imminent legal action pending for non-payment of debts
period of review limitations
court orders
settlements
inability to pay staff, or
other basic necessities for the business or people you are responsible for.

The financial need must be serious and immediate.

Evidence of hardship
Individual taxpayers

Evidence includes:

official eviction notice (not a warning of possible eviction due to rental arrears)
pending disconnection of essential services, like water, electricity, gas, mobile phone or internet
notice of impending legal action
letter from charitable organisation regarding

loss of employment, and/or
inability to provide for basic necessities
bank notice (for example, overdraft call or mortgaged property repossession. Bank statements or ATM receipts are not accepted as evidence)
overdue medical bills
letter from doctor verifying the inability to earn an income due to illness or caring for a sick family member
final notice from school regarding payment of mandatory fees
funeral expenses, or
repossession notice of essential items (for example car, motorcycle).
Taxpayers experiencing vulnerability

If you believe the taxpayer is experiencing vulnerability, see Tailored support for taxpayers experiencing vulnerability.
Opens in a new window

Business customers

Evidence includes:

bank notices (for example, overdraft call. Bank statements or ATM receipts are not accepted as evidence)
pending disconnection of essential services, like water, electricity, gas, mobile phone or internet
eviction notice
disconnection notice
repossession notice
notice of impending legal action
staff pay records
contract payment schedules, or
legal documents.
Natural disasters

Taxpayers impacted by natural disasters may request priority refund requests without evidence. They must claim 'natural disaster' in their cover letter.

How to request priority processing

Refer to Hardship - requesting priority processing
Opens in a new window
 to assist a taxpayer requesting priority processing of refunds due to hardship.

