Financial difficulties and serious hardship considerations

This scenario page provides possible indicators of financial difficulties or serious hardship.

Last published 25 March 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This scenario page provides possible indicators of financial difficulties or serious hardship.

A taxpayer experiencing serious hardship will not have the capacity to pay their primary tax debt now or within a reasonable timeframe.

Serious hardship is not defined by law but is defined in PS LA 2011/17
Opens in a new window
 - Debt relief, waiver and non-pursuit. It must be determined on a case by case basis.

Someone would be considered in serious hardship if they were deprived of what are considered necessities to normal community standards.

Recent updates
25/03/2026	Update to 'Serious hardship'	SD-102806


04/02/2026

	

Update to 'Financial difficulties' table, 'Income and employment' section.

	

SD-101711

Checks
Financial difficulties
Category	Factors to consider
Income and employment	

Current employment details.

Amount of income from employment.

Any other sources of income (including other shared household income if appropriate).

Recent changes to income and/or employment.

Details of any businesses, and whether provision is being made for ongoing tax
liabilities.

Have they been in jail?

Do they live in a city or regional area?


Assets	

It is reasonable to expect some assets be sold to pay a debt (e.g. land, shares, a boat).

Generally this would not include a family home of modest value, with little equity. 

Substantial equity in a home may indicate an ability to obtain finance.

Have they acquired any assets in preference to addressing their tax debt?


Necessary expenses	

Expenses can vary from taxpayer to taxpayer, depending on their circumstances. 

For example:

fuel costs are high in some regions

expenses may be incurred as a carer

costs of medications vary.


Compliance history and current level of engagement	

Attitude to the liability and preparedness to plan for future obligations must be considered.

If past compliance is poor, a change in behaviour must be observed.

How the debt arose is also relevant, particularly if it was raised after an audit.

What contributed to the non-compliance?

Was this reasonable in their circumstances?


Age	

Age may impact on issues such as:

likelihood of financial situation improving

future employment prospects

longer term capacity to pay a debt.


Family situation	

Marriage or relationship breakdown can severely impact the taxpayer's financial circumstances.

Often, they are unable to give their tax matters the necessary attention. 

Many suffer health issues as a result e.g. depression.


Dependants	

The amount the taxpayer can apply to payment of a debt is impacted by the number of dependants they are responsible for supporting.

Dependants having medical considerations can also affect their capacity.




Health

	

Health issues can be a major cause of debt and can affect ongoing ability to pay the debt. 

Some factors to consider are:

ability to earn and maintain income

likelihood of financial situation improving

medical expenses

mental health

alcohol, gambling or drug addiction.


Other debts	

Capacity to pay other debts and/or priority given to payment of those debts can impact on a decision.

Serious hardship

Hardship must be determined on a case by case basis. An individual is considered in serious hardship when they are unable to provide the following for themselves, their family or dependants. The following list is not exhaustive:

food 
accommodation 
clothing 
medical treatment 
education 
other basic necessities. 

A taxpayer may also be experiencing vulnerability, for more information see Tailored support for taxpayers experiencing vulnerability.
