Debt Hardship - Debt trained staff

Support for taxpayers experiencing financial difficulty and serious hardship.

Last published 29 June 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page:

is for Debt Assist Hardship trained staff only, and
provides the actions to take for taxpayers experiencing financial difficulty and serious hardship.

If you are speaking to a taxpayer who is experiencing financial hardship or financial difficulties, refer to Taxpayer facing financial difficulties or serious hardship.
Opens in a new window
  

If you are not trained and the taxpayer is experiencing:

financial difficulties or serious hardship, warm transfer to Hardship team x92176
immediate and severe vulnerability with threats of self-harm, family and domestic violence or coercion, warm transfer to Specialised Client Support team x92196.

Our core role is to collect tax. But we understand that life circumstances can make it hard for taxpayers to meet their tax obligations. Such as:

illness
financial hardship
family violence, or
trouble accessing online services.

While we seek to understand a taxpayer’s circumstances and provide appropriate support, we cannot:

change tax outcomes
remove tax debts, or
alter legislated obligations.

We will clearly communicate what we can and cannot do within the law and our role as the tax administrator.

Where obligations have been genuinely incurred and remain payable, our role is to listen with empathy, and support taxpayers to meet their obligations in the best way available, including through:

appropriate payment options
tailored engagement, and
referrals to additional support where relevant.
