Discuss available options

There are a range of support options available to help taxpayers experiencing hardship.

Some options have strict legislative or policy requirements and may not be available in all circumstances.

Use the table below to guide a transparent discussion of the available options, explain eligibility requirements clearly, and identify what support may be appropriate for the taxpayer’s situation.

Using the table below:

clearly explain the range of support options available for the taxpayer, and any key eligibility requirements
discuss which options may apply to the taxpayer’s circumstances and why, and
where possible, assist the taxpayer to get back on track or resolve their situation.

Some assistance options have strict requirements that must be met before they can be considered. Where an option is not available, clearly explain the reasons and discuss alternative support options.

When discussing support options, you should:

acknowledge that a range of support options exist, noting that not all options will apply in every circumstance
identify and discuss only the options that may be relevant to the taxpayer’s situation
for relevant options, explain what the option does and outline any key eligibility requirements or exclusions
clearly explain where an option is not available and why, and
describe the next step, including referrals or warm transfers where appropriate.

Assistance 

	

Details 




Payment plans 

	

A taxpayer experiencing hardship may need a tailored payment plan that suits their circumstances.

When establishing a payment plan, all debts (including debts not yet due but visible on the account) must be included into payment plans. It will cause any active plan to default if a debt on the account is not included in a payment plan.

Payment plans should aim to finalise the debt in the shortest reasonable timeframe, noting that general interest charge (GIC) continues to accrue while amounts remain unpaid.

In some circumstances, longer term or extended payment arrangements may be considered where this is necessary to support ongoing compliance. These arrangements should be based on the taxpayer’s capacity to pay and may require approval in line with the appropriate delegation.  

Go to Negotiating payment options.
Opens in a new window




Remission of penalties and GIC

	

Consider remission of penalties or interest taking into consideration their circumstances. Remission of penalties and interest is on a case-by-case basis.

Go to GIC, FTL, SIC remissions or cancellation request.
Opens in a new window




Release from debt 

	

Taxpayers experiencing financial hardship may be released from some, or all tax debt.

Advise them all lodgments are required to be finalised and up to date before release can be considered, this ensures the debt balance is accurate and up to date.

An individual or the trustee of a deceased person can apply for release. 

We cannot release debts for:

GST

PAYG withholding

Excess contribution tax (ECT)

Division 293 liabilities

Super guarantee charge (SGC)

Director penalty notices.

Go to Release enquiry
Opens in a new window
 to check if taxpayer’s debt(s) is eligible for release. 

