Operation Protego

You must check if the taxpayer is identified for Operation Protego activities before proceeding. To do this, check the taxpayer's Siebel record for a 2022 Operation Protego alert.

Operation Protego activities impact the advice provided and how taxpayers are treated. Taxpayers are not able to claim serious hardship in relation to GST Fraud.

Where the taxpayer has an Operation Protego debt on their account:

you must still consider their circumstances to assess if it is appropriate to exercise the discretion to refund and not offset
the assessment must be based on the individual merits of the request, and
the Operation Protego debt weighs heavily against the Commissioner granting them any form of support.

Where identified taxpayers claim financial hardship, refer them to external agencies listed in the Operation Protego Conversation Support Guide.
Opens in a new window

Correspondence requests

If you have received a Request Not To Offset (RNTO) activity via iWD and there has been an email sent to the external collection agencies (ECA), contact the operative that sent the email request before proceeding.

If you are actioning a correspondence request, you will need to contact the taxpayer
Opens in a new window
 to obtain the required information. Ensure you:

advise you are currently reviewing their request
clearly explain the definition of serious financial hardship
consider if the taxpayer has been impacted by exceptional circumstances.

For a definition of serious hardship, refer to the links under Background.

Initial checks and considerations

The Commissioner of Taxation (the Commissioner) must offset refunds and credits against taxation and other debts. This is stated under section 8AAZL of the Taxation Administration Act 1953 (TAA). In limited circumstances, tax law provides the Commissioner with a discretion to refund a credit, and not to offset the amount against a tax debt.

For more information go to:

PS LA 2011/21
Opens in a new window
 - Offsetting of refunds and credits against taxation and other debts
Offsetting.
Opens in a new window

Where the discretion at law to refund and not offset is enlivened, staff must consider whether it is appropriate to exercise the discretion:

on a case-by-case basis
having regard to the taxpayer’s specific circumstances.

The discretion not to offset is enlivened where the:

Commissioner has agreed to defer recovery of the debt
debt is
due but not payable, or
under a payment arrangement with which the taxpayer is complying
credit is
to be offset against a director penalty debt, or
a Family Tax Benefit (FTB) credit.

Where the request is relating to the reversal of an offset of a taxpayer's income tax refund against an FTB debt, refer to Reverse offset of ATO credit to FTB debt.
Opens in a new window

A request to exercise the discretion to refund and not offset the credit can be made:

once the credit has been input on the account, or
directly after the taxpayer has become aware that a credit is not to be refunded. For example, following receipt of an assessment notice which states the credit is to be applied against other amounts payable.

