D-1 - SIC remission decision guidelines and tools to assess remission requests.

Last published 28 April 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

D-1

Information to guide with the decision-making process when actioning a remission request for shortfall interest charge (SIC).

These guidelines will assist Frontline Compliance (FC) and Debt Assist staff when actioning non-audit related SIC remission requests.

The desired outcome is to achieve a decision that is consistent with both the:

law, and
PS LA 2006/8.
Opens in a new window

If it is fair and reasonable to do so, we may remit all or part of an amount of SIC. In exercising the powers of remission, we must consider the:

individual circumstances
the extent to which factors beyond the taxpayer's control were responsible for the size and duration of the shortfall
principle that remission should occur where the circumstances justify the Commonwealth bearing part or all the cost of delayed payments.
Recent updates
23/03/2026	Updated 'Narrating your decision' section.	SD-102757
27/02/2026	Updates to In Detail.	SD-102288
In Detail
Before you begin

FC and Debt Assist staff can action:

phone requests which meet all exceptions
correspondence requests for non-audit related SIC.

For more information, refer to Remission or cancellation initial checks.
Opens in a new window

Telephony requests for SIC remissions

You must complete the Telephony SIC remission application form
Opens in a new window
 and attach this to the Siebel activity for all telephony SIC remissions, excluding written requests where an outbound call is made for additional information (optional).

Open and save a copy of the Telephony SIC remission application form to your H:\ drive.

Client initiated amendment

Where a taxpayer makes a voluntary disclosure of a shortfall amount (often referred to as a self-amendment request), the disclosure itself is not a ground for remission.

A taxpayer, who has self-assessed incorrectly (even if they exercised reasonable care), should not end up in a more beneficial position than a taxpayer who has self-assessed, reported and paid correctly.

However, there may be some cases where the circumstances surrounding the voluntary disclosure will make it fair and reasonable to remit interest charges. Where remission on the basis of voluntary disclosure is considered appropriate, remission will generally only be to the base rate.

When considering any remission of SIC on the basis of voluntary disclosure, you must consider:

the timeliness of the disclosure after the error was first detected
whether the disclosure was made before
being told of the commencement of an examination, or
publication of a ATO initiative which may have led to the discovery of the shortfall by the Commissioner (remission is more unlikely if such notification or publication had occurred)
whether the Commonwealth in any way contributed to the taxpayer taking their original position
the size of the shortfall, either in
monetary terms, or
relation to the whole of the taxpayer's affairs, and
the taxpayer's compliance history, including the number of times a taxpayer has had to disclose shortfalls following an initial self-assessment of liability.
Amendments - CGT after Property settlement

Where a property contract is settled in a later part of an Income year, a taxpayer is required to include the capital gain or loss in the Income year the contract was made. They do not include the Capital gain/loss in the income year the contract was settled.

However, a taxpayer is not required to include any capital gain or loss in the appropriate year until the actual change of ownership occurs.

When settlement occurs, the taxpayer is then required to include any capital gain or loss in the Income year the contract was made.

If an assessment has already been made for the Income year, the taxpayer may need to amend the assessment. 

When an assessment is amended and a liability for interest arises, consideration to remit the interest must be made for each case on its own merit. It is expected however, discretion would ordinarily be exercised to remit the interest in full where:

requests for amendment are lodged, and

where relevant, self-amendments are made, within a reasonable time after the date of settlement.

In most cases, we would consider a period of one month after settlement to be a reasonable period.

Refer to Taxable Determination 94/89.
Opens in a new window

Consider taxpayer's circumstances
Info Alert
Information
Full or partial payment of outstanding primary tax is not mandatory for consideration of SIC remission request.

Where it is fair and reasonable to do so, you may remit all or part of the SIC. The extent of any remission must consider the:

individual circumstances of a case, and
extent to which factors beyond the taxpayer's control were responsible for the size and duration of the shortfall.
Circumstances	Considerations
Beyond the taxpayer's control
	

 To better understand taxpayer's circumstances, consider:

what caused the taxpayer's inability to lodge the correct information when the original return was lodged?

was the delay in lodging the correct information caused by the taxpayer?

was the taxpayer experiencing vulnerability that materially impacted their ability to comply?

did the taxpayer act within a reasonable timeframe to reduce the severity and impact of the circumstances? For example, by

notifying the ATO

keeping the ATO informed

calling in to negotiate a sustainable Payment Plan

making voluntary payments

the taxpayer is entitled to a remission of SIC where an early payment has been made

SIC should be reduced by the amount of any interest on early payment (IEP) that the taxpayer is entitled to

was the amendment made before being told of the commencement of an examination, or publication of a ATO initiative which may have led to the discovery of the shortfall by the Commissioner (remission is more unlikely if such notification or publication had occurred)?

was the amendment a one-off event?

does the taxpayer have a history of

lodging the incorrect information

amending the Tax return, and then

sending in a request for SIC remission?

did the Commonwealth in any way contribute to the taxpayer taking their original position?

the size of the shortfall, either in monetary terms or in relation to the whole of the taxpayer's affairs.


Taxpayer could not have been aware of shortfall when lodging return	

A shortfall amount may arise in situations where the taxpayer did not know and could not have known that a shortfall would arise when they lodged their original return.

This would occur where the return is correct, and it is only future events that trigger the need to adjust a liability. Examples of this include:

where a taxpayer becomes entitled to a receipt of compensation in a particular year, which may in some circumstances trigger an adjustment to capital proceeds and affect capital gains or losses in an earlier year's return

where member companies of a consolidated group are affected by the requirements of section 701-70 of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 (ITAA 1997), when the head company makes an election to consolidate after the member company has already lodged its income tax return for the year

where the Commissioner makes an amended assessment of Division 293 tax for an individual, after a superannuation provider amended

their Member Contribution Statement (MCS)

self-managed super fund annual report, and

the fund's reporting is outside the control of the individual.
(Remission will not be warranted if the fund is a self-managed superannuation fund and the individual is a trustee, as it would be considered that the individual should have been aware of the potential shortfall.).

Each case must be considered on its merits. In the above examples, it may be appropriate for you to grant full remission of interest charges related to the shortfall, usually on the condition that appropriate amendment requests are lodged within a reasonable time after the need to amend arises, if required. The remission does not automatically apply in the event the taxpayer alleges they mistakenly applied the law.

The circumstances must:

be fair and reasonable to give rise to the remission, and

ensure that Commonwealth is not unfairly bearing part or all of the cost of delayed payments.

Making a decision

Considering the taxpayer's circumstances, relevant practice statement, resources, and desired outcome, decide:

whether the remission should be granted or refused
if granted, whether it should be a full or partial remission. Partial remission should be considered when the circumstances do not warrant a full remission.
Partial remission of the SIC

A partial remission of the SIC occurs when a decision is made to remit part, but not fully remit, the SIC applied to a document. A partial remission may be appropriate when full remission is not warranted.

Where remission of SIC is sought for multiple documents, a decision to remit the full SIC on some, but not all, of the documents in the request is not considered a partial remission.

Partial remission example

An individual is liable to SIC for amendments of income tax returns for 2018, 2019 and 2020. A decision is made to remit part of the SIC on each income tax return. This constitutes a partial remission decision.

Full remission example

If the decision was made to:

remit the SIC for the 2018 income tax return in full
not remit the SIC for the 2019 and 2020 income tax returns.

This decision would not be classified as a partial remission, but as:

a full remission for the 2018 income tax return, and
no remission on the other documents.
Narrating your decision

When granting or refusing a remission, narrate how you made the decision referencing the prescribed grounds in the PS LA 2006/8.
Opens in a new window

You must explain how you have arrived at your decision in considering:

are circumstances beyond the taxpayer's control?
were the circumstances responsible for the size and duration of the shortfall?
does the taxpayer have a history of lodging the incorrect information, amending the Tax return, and then sending in a request for SIC remission?
did the taxpayer act within a reasonable timeframe?

For partial remission, you must also include:

why the circumstances do not warrant a full remission of the dollar amount, and
how the approved amount was calculated.
Penalty remission - Cancellation macro

The macro will not gather SIC, where applicable manually check for SIC amounts.

