D-1 - FTL penalty remission decision guidelines and tools to assess remission requests.

Last published 23 March 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

D-1

This page shows the decision-making process when actioning a request for remission of failure to lodge on time (FTL) penalties.

When making a remission decision, you should be aware of the reasons why we have a FTL penalty:

revenue collections are often contingent on timely lodgments
PAYG withholding annual reports and STP data reconcile amounts withheld from employees. Failing to lodge these can prevent individuals from lodging correctly using prefilling
STP data aids the ATO’s ability to identify employers not meeting their SG and PAYGW liability, or who did not onboard to STP by the legislated due date. This data is also shared with other government agencies. The effect of missing or incomplete STP data is that it impacts
community services, and
the benefits to employees who are mutual taxpayers
businesses may gain cash-flow advantages by delaying lodgment (by delaying updates to PAYG instalment rates)
information from large entities is valuable to the ATO in managing compliance risks
partly as cost recovery where the ATO has undertaken compliance action to bring the lodgment in.

The desired outcome is to achieve a fair and reasonable decision that is consistent with both the:

law, and
PS LA 2011/19
Opens in a new window
- Administration of the penalty for failure to lodge on time.

Taxpayers will need to show it is fair and reasonable to grant the remission, considering the nature of the specific event. Not only must the exercise of power to remit be fair to the taxpayer, it must also:

align to PS LA 2011/19
Opens in a new window
address unfair financial advantage
uphold community expectations.
Recent updates
23/03/2026	Updated 'Narrating your decision' section.	

SD-102756

In Detail
Debt referred to external collection agency (ECA)

Do not update or make changes to the ECA case summary ID or referral activity in Siebel. Doing so will withdraw the taxpayer from the ECA.

Where you identify that a taxpayer has been referred to an ECA
Opens in a new window
 remission requests can still be considered by the ATO, including request made via:

telephony
correspondence, and
online.

Before actioning any FTL penalty remission requests, complete the checks outlined in:

Telephony request - GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation
Opens in a new window
 or
Correspondence request - GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation.
Opens in a new window
Telephony requests for FTL remission

You must complete the Telephony FTL remission application form
Opens in a new window
 and attach this to the Siebel activity for all telephony FTL remissions, excluding written requests where an outbound call is made for additional information (optional).

Open and save a copy of the Telephony FTL remission application form to your H:\ drive.

Consider the taxpayer's circumstances

A remission must not be granted:

when there is insufficient information for a decision to be made. Contact should be made with the taxpayer to get further information to make a decision
to encourage payments of debts
to finalise a case where the ATO has not tried to collect the overdue amount, or
where the reasons provided by the taxpayer are only remotely linked to the late or non-lodgment.

A remission may be granted when the taxpayer was unable to lodge on time.

Where circumstances for late lodgment were...
	Then...
beyond the taxpayer's control	

to better understand taxpayer's circumstances, consider the following as appropriate:

what caused late lodgment

how this affected their ability to lodge

did it affect their ability to lodge for the full length of time

could they have lodged any sooner

how those circumstances could not be predicted, and

why they or their agent were not in a position to request further time to lodge.

Examples of circumstances which may impact the taxpayers ability to lodge include:

flood, fire or other natural disaster

serious illness of the taxpayer and there was no other person available to lodge the form, or

blocked access to records, e.g. records seized during police search, or retained as evidence in a court matter.


within the taxpayer's control	

it may still be appropriate to remit the penalty if:

it is fair and reasonable to do so, and

consideration is made on other factors, such as

the taxpayer's compliance history

the length of time the document was overdue

the taxpayer's circumstances

the agent's circumstances (where applicable), and

any relevant contact made to the ATO before the document due date.

Fair and reasonable to remit

Even if circumstances were not beyond the entity's control, it may still be appropriate for you to remit the penalty (in full or in part) where you can make a decision that it is reasonable to do so.

The onus is on the entity to show that it is fair and reasonable to remit, considering:

the nature of the specific event, or
decision that prevented lodgment.

Examples where remission would ordinarily be appropriate (not exhaustive) where the taxpayer:

or their agent was sick with a severe life-threatening illness, such as battling cancer
was caring for another person who was sick with a severe life-threatening illness
could not lodge as they had not received information from other parties such as employers (a payment summary for example) that would enable them to lodge - ideally, the taxpayer should be able to demonstrate they have persistently tried to get this information
was affected by a disaster such as fire or flood or state of emergency which took their complete attention and perhaps meant that some records were lost.

Examples where remission would ordinarily not be appropriate (not exhaustive), where the taxpayer:

claims they could not lodge on time because they were on holiday
claims they could not lodge on time because they were extremely busy
could not lodge because they were sick with a cold (or other short-lived non-severe illness)
requests remission because they claim that they did not receive any reminders to lodge from the ATO
has not yet lodged the document.
Making decision

Considering the taxpayer's circumstances, relevant practice statement, resources, and desired outcome to decide:

whether the remission should be granted or refused, and
if granted, whether it should be a full or partial remission. Partial remission should be considered when the circumstances do not warrant a full remission.
Partial remission of the FTL penalty

A partial remission of the FTL penalty occurs when a decision is made to reduce, but not fully remit, the penalty applied to a document. A partial remission may be appropriate when full remission is not warranted.

Where remission of FTL penalty is sought for multiple documents, a decision to remit the full FTL penalty on some, but not all, of the documents in the request is not considered a partial remission.

Partial remission example

A small entity is liable to FTL penalty for late lodgment of activity statements for the September 2018, December 2018 and March 2019 quarters. The penalty amounts to 5 penalty units for each activity statement. A decision is made to reduce the penalty amount by 4 penalty units on each activity statement. This constitutes a partial remission decision.

Full remission example

If the decision was made to:

remit the September 2018 activity statement penalty in full
not remit the December 2018 and March 2019 activity statement penalties.

This decision would not be classified as a partial remission, but as:

a full remission for the September 2018 activity statement, and
no remission on the other documents.
Narrating your decision

When granting or refusing a remission, narrate how you made the decision referencing the prescribed grounds in the PS LA 2011/19.
Opens in a new window

You must explain how you have arrived at your decision in considering:

was the FTL penalty caused by circumstances beyond the taxpayer's control?
could those circumstances be predicted?
were they or their agent in a position to request further time to lodge?
where circumstances are within the taxpayer's control, is it fair and reasonable to remit and why? For example, it's not fair and reasonable to remit because <____>.

For partial remission, you must also include, why the circumstances do not warrant a remission of the full penalty.

Penalty Remission-Cancellation macro

Gathers interest details for a taxpayer including

Corporate penalty system

ICP, and
previous remissions in last 5 years.

Allows an officer to indicate which entries they want to remit.

May not gather FTL penalties imposed over 5 years.

When undertaking a remission, the macro
Opens in a new window
 will check

APS level and authorisations

amount of debt and payment arrangement status, and

overdue lodgments.

Where the total of FTL penalty applied and previously remitted for a period is zero, no further remission or cancellation is required.
