Non-pursuit re-raise (Frontline Compliance and Debt Assist)

Procedure for re-raising debts on hold for both non-pursuit transactions and non-pursuable debt accounting treatment types.

Last published 21 July 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page provides the procedure for re-raising debts on hold (formerly known as Non-pursued debt) for both ‘non-pursuit’ transactions and ‘non-pursuable debt’ accounting treatment types.

Warning
alert:
This procedure is for use by Frontline Compliance and Debt Assist staff only.

FS staff are to use the Non-pursuit re-raise (ISC staff)
Opens in a new window
 procedure.

Reasons for re-raising

Re-raising might occur for a number of reasons:

The taxpayer is under an insolvent administration and a dividend has been declared.

The insolvency administrator requested breakdown of employee superannuation entitlements.

It is required to offset any credits on the account, including

lodgment credits (through automated system processes)

remission or adjustment of any non-pursued penalty or interest

credit amendment or cancellation of any non-pursued assessment

payment of non-pursued amounts

a washup credit has been applied to an assessment after it has been non-pursued, and

stale cheque re-credited to the account.

It is required to prevent an incorrect refund from issuing.

The non-pursuit amount is for a credit and the taxpayer has since re-engaged.

There is new evidence recovery action is now viable, for example the taxpayer

is employed

has realisable assets

was previously untraceable and has now been located.

