Scenarios
Automatic offsetting to debts on hold

You may identify credits offset to debts on hold through an automated system process. The debts on hold may have been fully or partially re-raised.

Where a debt on hold remains, discuss taxpayers capacity to pay and payment options.

If the taxpayer does not have an active debt, and they do not have the ability to repay the balance of remaining debt on hold amounts, re-raise may not be appropriate. You may choose to leave this amount on hold.

Ensure the taxpayer understands what the debt being on hold means, that it remains payable and further credits may be used to offset.
If you action a manual re-raise, trigger a statement of account to issue to the taxpayer so they are aware the debt has been re-raised.

Where the taxpayer has both an active debt and a debt on hold, a full re-raise may be actioned if the taxpayer can address the total debt.

Consider what other assistance may be appropriate to help them repay the full balance. This may include:

remission of penalties and general interest charge (GIC) as appropriate
payment plan options.
Invalid credit

If a period is in credit and there is a non-pursuit posting with it, GIC may re-calculate incorrectly and result in a false credit situation.

These credits are to remain untouched, not offset or moved to allow for an invalid credit review item to generate overnight.

If a false credit situation is identified or suspected at any stage during the re-raise and you are:

