Transfer types
Payment transfer

A payment transfer may be required where a payment has been credited to the wrong account due to taxpayer or ATO error.

Partial payment transfer

A partial payment transfer may be required if a:

taxpayer has made a lump sum payment into one account, and the payment was intended for 2 (or more) different accounts
full payment was made and then system used this payment to partially offset another debt.

A transfer cannot be actioned for the purpose of a refund.

Payments can be transferred to accounts not in debt.

Credit transfer

Note: If the taxpayer is a deceased estate (individual/trust), you must only transfer credits to offset debits within the estate (e.g. trust to individual or individual to trust). Do not transfer credit to entities outside the estate.

A genuine credit is an amount which is raised on a taxpayer's account, resulting from a credit assessment (for example, lodgment of an activity statement or income tax return). A credit cannot be transferred for the purpose of a refund.

GIC credits are not genuine credits. If a GIC posting results in a credit balance, complete a reconciliation of the account to confirm the original credit or payment source. Credits that are not genuine cannot be transferred.

