FTL penalty remission - Reasons for refusal

FTL penalty remission refusal examples used to complete Reason for Decision field for Adv FTL penalty remission decision letter.

Last published 6 October 2025

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page contains failure to lodge (FTL) penalty remission refusal examples. These can be used by operatives completing the Reason for Decision field in the Adv FTL penalty remission decision letter.

Recent Updates
06/10/2025 	Page re written. 	SD-97876 
22/01/2025	Update to 'Financial hardship related issues'.	SD-89550
In Detail

When refusing a FTL penalty remission request:

be clear and concise when explaining the reason for your decision

if applicable, you may include multiple reasons, ensuring the sentence structure remains logical and easy to follow.

The most common remission scenarios are outlined in the following paragraphs. While you may use them as a guide, it is not mandatory to use them verbatim. Tailor the wording to reflect the taxpayer’s individual circumstances.

If there are additional circumstances to consider:

tailor your response accordingly, and

reference the remission principles outlined in the Law Administration Practice Statement PS LA 2011/19
Opens in a new window
 – Administration of the penalty for failure to lodge on time.

Ensure you:

carefully review the text before finalising

fill in all required details where < > appears

remove the < > brackets

edit the document as needed to suit the context. 

Standard paragraphs

The following paragraphs may be a suitable starting point and should be tailored to the taxpayer’s specific circumstances.

No circumstances provided for late lodgment
This paragraph may be suitable where the taxpayer has not provided any circumstances for late lodgment.

Refusal example:

'You have not demonstrated how circumstances prevented you from lodging by the due date'.

Did not request further time to lodge
This paragraph may be suitable when the taxpayer didn’t request extra time or didn’t request extra time within a reasonable period and hasn’t demonstrated they were unable to do so.

Refusal example:
'You did not request additional time to lodge <within a reasonable timeframe> and have not demonstrated that you were unable to do so'.
Agent issues (does not meet safe harbour criteria)
This paragraph may be suitable when the circumstances involve agent delays, and the taxpayer has not met the safe harbour requirements.

Refusal example:

'While you have indicated that issues with your agent affected your ability to lodge on time, it remains your responsibility to manage your affairs to meet the lodgment due date. Reasons related to agent delays do not justify penalty remission'.
Lack of time or reminders 
This paragraph can be applied where the taxpayer has cited a lack of time or lack of reminders from the ATO as the reason for the delayed lodgment.

Refusal example:

'You can request a lodgment deferral at any stage. Reasons related to <lack of time / lack of reminders received from the ATO> do not justify penalty remission'.
Financial hardship related issues - cannot pay agent

This paragraph can be applied where the taxpayer has mentioned an inability to pay their agent as the reason for the delayed lodgment.

Refusal example:

'Although you have stated financial circumstances have impacted your capacity to lodge, you can self-lodge or access support by way of government funded Tax Help and National Tax Clinic programs at no cost.

Visit the National Tax Clinic program
Opens in a new window
 or search National Tax Clinics to:

check if you are eligible, and
find a tax clinic that may be able to assist.
Impeded access to records/information with no action taken

This paragraph may be suitable where lodgment was delayed as the taxpayer did not have access to records/information from other parties and they have not shown they persistently tried to get this information.

Refusal example:

'While limited access to records or information from other parties may have contributed to the delay in lodging, you have not provided sufficient evidence of consistent efforts to obtain this information'.



Not fair and reasonable - circumstances within control

This paragraph may be suitable when the delay was within the taxpayer’s control and they haven’t shown that remission is fair or reasonable.

Refusal example:

'You have not prioritised your tax obligations. It is your responsibility to manage your affairs to ensure lodgment by the due date. Remission would not be considered fair or reasonable when other taxpayers have taken steps to lodge on time'.

Not onboarded to Single Touch Payroll

Use this paragraph when a taxpayer has a not onboarded to single touch payroll by the mandated date:

1 July 2018 - for employers with more than 20 employees, and
1 July 2019 - for employers with less than 20 employees.

Refusal example:

'The decision to onboard after the penalty has been applied is not grounds for penalty remission'.

