Siebel - Secure message guidelines and principles
Siebel - Secure message guidelines and principles

The guidelines and principles for using Unstructured Mail Message function in Siebel.

Last published 12 September 2025

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page provides the principles and guidelines for using Unstructured Mail Message in Siebel to send a secure message to a: 

registered agent's Practice mail in Online services for agents (OSfA), or
taxpayer's Secure mail in Online services for business (OSB).  

Tax professionals and businesses access ATO online services via a secure link from the ATO website to:

view account transactions
request refunds
update registration details
lodge certain forms
send secure messages.

ATO online services are protected by myID and Relationship Authorisation Manager (RAM).
Opens in a new window
 Business is conducted within this secure environment.

This page does not cover the Provision of Written Advice function within ATO online services and its interaction with the Technical Decision Making System (TDMS).
Recent updates
12/09/2025	

Update throughout the page.

	SD-96751
In Detail
Externally initiated secure messages

OSfA and OSB allow registered agents and taxpayers to send secure messages to the ATO. These secure messages are called Unstructured correspondence. They are in a free text, rather than a structured template format.

As the messages are free text, they tend to be constructed:

in a less formal manner than traditional written correspondence
similar to email.

A secure message sent to the ATO can contain:

up to 2000 characters
attachments. 

All characters on the QWERTY keyboard can be used in messages to the ATO except<>.

Images of both the attachments and the secure message are viewable via Image Viewer.

ATO-initiated secure messages

Staff can initiate unstructured correspondence via online services. This means that you can initiate a secure message from Siebel to a taxpayer or registered agent:

via their online services mailbox
whether or not a message has been received from them. 

When you create a secure message:

an outbound activity is created in Siebel
it is sent to the recipient's online services mailbox
you can add any necessary attachments.

Refer to Siebel - initiate a secure message via online services
Opens in a new window
 on how to send a secure message from Siebel.

Inbound online services interaction with Siebel

When a secure message is sent to the ATO via online services, a Siebel activity is created. The interaction is shown as either:

Business Portal (BP), or 
Tax Agent Portal (TAP).

If the sender is:

an OSB user, the inbound activity will be shown on that business' Client Contact History (CCH)
a registered agent, the inbound activity will show

in the Tax Practitioner view, and
on the taxpayer's CCH, if the taxpayer was in focus when the registered agent sent the message.
Replying to an externally initiated secure message

Siebel allows for a reply to be sent back to the sender who initiated the original message. The reply is sent via the channel it was received through.

The Reply function in the inbound activity is used to create a:

service request, and 
new outbound activity. 

The Unstructured Mail Message field is enabled in the outbound activity. This allows an ATO officer to write their reply. This reply is then sent from Siebel to the recipient's online services mailbox.

If the reply is sent to:

an OSB user, the reply will be shown on that business' CCH
 registered agent, the reply will be shown in

the Tax Practitioner's CCH, and 
if applicable, their client's CCH.

When sending a reply, you need to ensure that you are sending it:

to the authorised contact, and 
through the correct channel, either OSB or OSfA.
Unstructured mail message field

The Unstructured Mail Message field:

is used for
replying to inbound secure messages, or 
initiating secure messages
has a 2000 character limit which limits the message capacity 
does not contain official branding or signature blocks
is a free text field only and has no inbuilt templates. 

Characters on a standard QWERTY keyboard, including special characters such as < ? and &, can be used in messages. Ensure chevrons <> indicating variable text are removed before sending.

Secure mail message protocol

As secure messages are less formal than traditional written correspondence, registered agents and taxpayers may assume that an informal message will be received. However, when preparing written messages to registered agents and taxpayers, you should still refer to:

the ATO Outbound Interactions Editing Guide,
This link will download a file
 and 
Letters, emails, SMS and myGov messages in the ATO style guide.
This link will download a file

While there are no specific ATO guidelines for online services secure messages, avoid inappropriate informality when using the Unstructured Mail Message field.

The Unstructured Mail Message function allows a message of only 2000 characters. This limit means you must decide if it is more appropriate to:

add an attachment, or 
send a Desktop or Manual System Correspondence (MSC) letter as well as, or instead of, the secure mail message.

The decision to send a message within the 2000 character limit or send a more formal piece of correspondence will depend on the type of recipient.

You cannot add a hyperlink (or URL) into a secure mail message.

Some examples of responses are provided below. Refer to your business line procedures and policy documents for further guidance.

When adding an attachment to a secure mail message, the wording should refer to the attachment in the message. If sending a desktop or manual letter, you should advise the registered agent or taxpayer of the channel if the letter is being sent through, for example:

online services or 
mail.
Examples of responses

The following examples show when a more formal approach should be taken, as well as sending a secure message in response to the inbound request. 

ATO-initiated messages are not covered in these examples.

Example 1

The request is for a transfer of payments between a taxpayer's Income Tax (IT) and Fringe Benefits Tax (FBT) accounts. You complete the transactions in the correct systems.

You should advise the requestor using only an Unstructured Mail Message. The main text of the message should read: 'Transfer completed as requested'.

Example 2

A request is for a remission of a general interest charge (GIC) for late payment of a taxpayer's IT account. You decide that a remission is not warranted in the circumstances provided. Any refusal of a GIC request must:

provide full details of the reason for the refusal 
outline the taxpayer's review rights.

The lack of appropriate ATO branding and the 2000 character limit will mean that you cannot send only a secure mail message in reply to the request.

You should send a secure mail message advising that a:

decision has been made, and 
formal response is attached. 

The main text of the message should read: 'We have made a decision in this matter and the formal response is attached'.

Salutation

The ATO is committed to using gender-neutral salutations and sign-offs in:

letters
emails, and 
SMS. 

This means we no longer use 'Dear Sir/Madam', 'Dear Mr' or 'Dear Ms'. There are a number of options to choose from. The appropriate choice depends on:

the type of correspondence
its purpose, and 
whether or not you know the recipient's name.

Refer to Letters, emails, SMS and myGov messages in the ATO style guide.
This link will download a file

For tax professionals, use:

Dear <Agent's name>
Dear Tax Professional
To whom it may concern.

Refer to ATO Outbound Interactions Editing Guide.
This link will download a file

Signature block

As the contact details of the person responding to the enquiry appear in the secure mail message, a formal signature block is not required.

Place the following statement at the end of each secure message except where you are advised not to by your business line procedures: 'Thank you for using ATO online services.'

Unauthorised interactions

There will be times where a secure message has been initiated by an unauthorised contact. That is, they are not listed on ATO systems as an authorised contact. The initiator may be either an OSfA or OSB user.

Check for authorised contacts:

in the Authorised Contacts tab in ICP
Contact details, and 
Client links screens. 

If the author of the inbound secure message is not an authorised contact, no action should be taken to:

update taxpayer accounts, or 
provide taxpayer information.

A reply should be sent to advise that:

they are not an authorised contact
no action can be taken.

To prevent a breach of the secrecy provisions and Privacy Act, Proof of Identity (POI) processes must be followed before any reply is sent to the sender. You must ensure that the privacy of the authorising entity is not compromised.

Cross-entity authorisation - agent for tax purposes

Users of OSB have access to a function that allows cross-entity authorisation. This is managed within the Online Access Manager tool. It is known as appointing an agent for tax purposes.

An agent for tax purposes:

is not a registered agent but an OSB user granted authority to view the accounts and transact on behalf of other entities who are also OSB users
is considered a representative of the business
is often appointed where tax administration functions are performed centrally by one entity on behalf of other entities, such as a
consolidated group
business with multiple branches
small business that comprises a few related entities
has the authority within OSB to act in the capacity of another business
any action taken will be assumed to have been completed by the authorising business
an authorising business is legally liable for anything the agent for tax purposes does on its behalf.

Example

Company A, Company B and Company C are part of a corporate group whose tax functions have been centralised within Company A. 

Company B and Company C appoint Company A as their agent for taxation purposes using the Online Access Manager tool.

Company A can:

act as an agent for tax purposes on behalf of Company B and Company C within OSB
view the accounts of, and transact on behalf of Company B and Company C. 

Company B and Company C cannot view the accounts of, or transact on behalf of, Company A or each other.

What functions can access be given to?

An OSB user may grant an agent for tax purposes access to all or some of the following functions:

view account details
submit requests for 
refund 
transfer
view business registration details
update business registration details
add and update financial institution details
view existing activity statements
prepare and lodge activity statements
lodge revised activity statements
access other user's mail (secure messages). 

When an entity is transacting on behalf of another entity it uses its own authentication details. 

Example

Using the above example, this means that if Company A sends a secure message asking for action to be taken on Company B's account, the message is received and displayed on the CCH of Company A, not Company B.

Any reply sent to the message, will be recorded against Company A's CCH. This is even though it relates to Company B.

Privacy considerations

When an entity is appointed an agent for tax purposes, it may not be granted access to all of the functionality available within OSB. The ATO is not notified:

when an agent for tax purposes is appointed via OSB 
what functionality the agent has been appointed for.

You must ensure that the privacy of the authorising entity is not compromised. Check that the initiator of the secure message is listed as an authorised contact within ATO systems. Refer to Unauthorised interactions above.

You may note that the ABN and business name (Company C) mentioned in the body of the message is not the one displayed on the activity (Company A). This may also be the case with the header information of the message. As with general Inbound Correspondence, you may need to re-index the activity to Company C. 

To maintain the integrity of the online services interaction, re-indexing of the activity must not be undertaken. Follow Siebel - reply to a secure message via online services.
Opens in a new window

Attachments

Attachments can be sent with a secure message.

Some examples of these are:

worksheets created in Excel
letters generated outside of Siebel, for example, those created in BulLetS
Siebel desktop letters. That is, letters that are completed via an info path form and produce an immediate electronic image for local printing
an existing scanned image.
Attachment guidelines

Acceptable file types are: 

.doc
.pdf
.rtf
.xls
.tif
.jpg
.zip
.bmp
.mpp
.ppt
.png
.gif.

Each attachment can be no larger than 6MB:

a warning will show in Siebel when an attachment is larger than 2MB but smaller than 6MB
documents larger than 6MB will not pass through the ATO security protocols
a fatal error message will show if you try to attach a document of 6MB or larger.

Some online services users may not be able to access attachments greater than 2MB due to technology constraints (for example, their type of internet connection).

The attachment name must not be longer than 50 characters. This includes the file extension (for example, '.pdf', '.xls').

Documents with a security rating higher than Sensitive cannot be sent via online services.

Accessibility and usability issues can impact some visually impaired online services users. Documents in '.pdf' format are:

not readily accessible 
incompatible with screen-reader software. 

If visually impaired users cannot access the .pdf document, they need to contact the ATO to advise that they need to receive online services correspondence in Rich Text Format (.rtf). Procedures are in place within ATO online services to encourage external users to advise they are visually impaired when sending a secure message to the ATO.

Refer to the Outbound correspondence overview
Opens in a new window
 to:

send a desktop or manual system generated correspondence item instead of, or as well as, the secure message 
reply to the unstructured correspondence, or
send an ATO-initiated secure correspondence.

The Manual System and Desktop Correspondence function via online services will not be available until further notice.

