Quality Notes

Guidelines to ensure notes are of an acceptable standard.

Last published 25 May 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page is a guideline to ensure notes are of an acceptable standard.

Legal and business requirements place the onus on you to ensure your actions, contacts and decisions are professionally and appropriately noted. Notes are a reliable record of ATO interactions with taxpayers.

Quality notes are important because:

we rely on the notes of previous officers to enable us to make quality decisions
external stakeholders may require them, for example the Tax Ombudsman
they may be required as evidence in court proceedings
they justify and substantiate your actions, and
they may be requested and read by the taxpayer.
Recent updates
25/05/2026	Updates throughout page, new note template.	SD-104735
In Detail
Determine if note required
When to add a note

A note must be added to a taxpayer's record when:

you are unable to action a work item due to lack of available support or system issues, and need to include any information that may later be important to process or progress the activity
you are assigned the activity at the end of your shift, or are rolled onto telephony
completing PORO and cold transferring a call, for example items of proof and DVS items obtained
actioning a form and a suspense item reports, for example a tax return
providing account specific information or advice, for example account details
pre-legal warnings have been provided or taxpayer/ tax agent advised they do not want/need to hear the warnings
taking action or updating an account, where a suitable ICP adjustment reason or a Siebel finalisation code cannot be used to indicate this
escalating via email, or non-phone escalation to another area for action/consideration, where an account has been accessed and PORO completed
adjusting an accidental error made, for example accidentally terminating and then having to reissue an activity statement
a contact fails PORO, and you have concerns about the taxpayer's privacy or believe the contact was intending to breach privacy, for example contact pretended they were the taxpayer
the contact specifically requests a note to be added or has provided information that you feel should be noted, for example relative calling to inform that the taxpayer's obligations cannot be met due to sudden illness
updating or deleting a phone number
cold transferring and the taxpayers name cannot be selected in the Siebel contact name field
a verbal declaration has been obtained as required by a procedure.
Adding a note when unable to action a work item

If you are unable to action a work item, add a note using the No action able to be taken template in the Notes fly-out tab. Refer to Note templates for more information on fly-outs.

Siebel note fields

All notes for a taxpayer can be viewed by navigating to the Client Contact History (CCH) and reviewing the Notes field in each activity. Notes should be left on the activity that you are actioning.

There are different fields in Siebel where different information for a note can be recorded. Information captured in all these fields is subject to Freedom of Information (FOI) principles. These fields include:

Notes
Description
Summary.

If applicable, you must add your note before attaching the Case ID of a Siebel debt case to the activity. This ensures the note is transferred to AIS and is viewable in the Client Notes screen.

Notes field

This field is viewable in the Activity list and Activity details view. If there are multiple notes saved for an activity, the Notes field will default to display the latest created.

The Notes field:

allows up to 1500 characters to be captured

can only be edited for the notes you have created while the activity is open (once set to Done it cannot be edited)

has no limit to the number of separate notes that can be saved to one activity

Activity list view you can view the entire contents of the note by

hanging the mouse over the field, or

clicking in the field. Clicking in the Notes field will enable the MVG button.

The MVG button is automatically displayed in the Notes field. If the MVG button is selected in the Activity list or Activity details view, the Notes dialog box will display. This lists all notes associated with the activity. All notes in relation to the activity should be reviewed before taking action.

From the Notes dialog box, a new note can be created, or you can check for an existing note.

Fields available in the Notes dialog box are:

Activity Id: displays the current Siebel activity Id

Note Id: combination of letters and numbers which make up a unique ID

Notes: displays the text captured in the note

Created: displays the date and time that the note was created

Created by: displays the User ID of the officer who created the note

Last modified by: displays the User ID of the officer who created the note.

If you view a Work Management activity while working in a case, any notes written in the Notes field of the Work Management activity will be viewable in the Comments field.

Description field

This field is viewable in the Activity list and Activity details view.

The Description field:

should not include

taxpayer specific notes
'return to User ID xxxx'

is used to record administrative text in relation to an activity. Administrative text is used for internal purposes only and does not need to be retained

allows up to 150 characters to be captured

information can be deleted or altered when the activity is active

can be expanded by clicking on the icon (blank page symbol) located in the top right-hand corner

is for internal purposes only and does not need to be retained, for example, information to reclassify an activity.

The text should be relevant to the interaction to best describe the activity. This will assist in improving the ability to be able to search for the right interaction, when necessary.

For example, type:

DPN - if an inbound phone call relates to an enquiry for a Director Penalty Notice that has issued

Lodgment, Summons, S459E, Warning Letter, or Progress of Return, where the enquiry relates to those actions.

Summary field

This field is viewable in the Service request view. Taxpayer specific notes should not be left in the Summary field.

The Summary field:

is used to record administrative text in relation to a service request. Administrative text is used for internal purposes only and does not need to be retained

allows up to 100 characters to be captured

information can be deleted or altered

text box can be expanded by clicking on the icon (blank page symbol) located in the top right-hand corner.

Content of a quality note

A quality note must:

contain new information and enhance subsequent interactions
be clear and concise
contain no jargon
be professional and accurate
exclude all
personal comment
unsupported opinions
assumptions
be informative, including reasons for decisions made
be gender neutral. See 'Gender-neutral language' on ATO style guide.
Opens in a new window
Use of names

When adding a name to a note, use complete names and relationship. At the first usage, add the full name, for example, as John Smith (primary contact), subsequently use only John. Names must be added to the note:

every time you
interact with a taxpayer where the contact's name cannot be selected in the Siebel Contact name field
take action on a case
when additional third parties enter into the interaction.
Correct format

The table below provides the format to use, along with examples when creating notes in Siebel.

All approving officers must add a note, regardless of how approval was sought, including:

their decision
reasons for approving or rejecting the request
full name and APS level (Position number required for approvals in the case of non-pursuit work types only).
Required details
	Required content



Taxpayer details if applicable.

For example, where the contact's name cannot be selected in the Siebel contact name field.

	

Include the contact's full name and relationship to the entity or individual.

For example, John Smith (primary contact), subsequently use only John.




Brief description of the interaction.

	

For example, Remission of General Interest Charge of <insert amount>.




Decision or action taken including reason or justification.

	

For example, I have taken into consideration this company/taxpayer circumstances are <insert reason/impacts>. The reason/s I have granted this remission are <insert reasons>. This amount is within my delegated authority.

Outsource and labour hire staff must state their delegated authority.

Debt Assist staff must also include:

details of attempted contact (if applicable)
a short summary of previous actions
key facts
key decisions and the reasons for those decisions, including the supporting policy
an indication of proposed future action (if appropriate)
details of documents issued, and
elements and amounts of the debt included in your action.



Additional information.

	

Any relevant additional information:

any note templates
legal warnings provided
mandatory reference to legislation or additional or subsequent action taken
call dropped out
where approvals are verbally sought, approving officer's decision and reason, APS level and full name
where directed to include certain information.

Do not include additional information where decisions have not been made and/or where it does not enhance subsequent interactions.

Debt Assist staff are required to record all attempted contact with taxpayers, their solicitor, tax agent or other representatives.

When taking firmer action, staff must record the:

time of the attempted contact (where not already apparent) including details of any messages left for the taxpayer
name, phone number and position of the person who took the message
date of the call, when the notes are not recorded on the same day of the call.
Note templates

The note page templates are located in the Notes fly-out tab. Note page templates are created to support record consistency and quality notes. A note template will:

already contain some of the necessary information
contain headings to prompt for relevant information, and
clearly show where information needs to be added or edited.

EKM scripting will state when to use a note template to add a note. Templates will only appear if they are required for the page. When scripting states to add a note using a template:

select
Notes button at the top of the EKM page
Template tab
Assigned Templates drop-down menu and select the relevant template
Use template
edit the template as necessary
select Copy once the note is ready, and
paste (Ctrl + V) the note where required, for example, into your Siebel activity note.
