Recommend non-pursuit

Procedures for recommending non-pursuit as a method of clearing debt.

Last published 13 July 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

Information

Use the Recommend non-pursuit - QuickNav
Opens in a new window
 to get a tailored view of the content based on the query/activity you are actioning.

This page provides procedures for recommending non-pursuit: 

Non-pursuit (irrecoverable at law) is the method of reducing the debt where there is a legal avenue to do so. For example, deregistered company or the finalisation of an insolvency 
Non-pursuit (uneconomical to pursue) places a debt on hold. Placing a debt on hold should occur when the ATO has decided it is not cost effective to collect the debt at the time. Most debts placed on hold is actioned by the system and the taxpayer is not aware. When staff make the decision to place a debt on hold the taxpayer should not be notified. Debt can be taken off hold if we consider their circumstance has changed.

Debts placed on hold will:

remain included in the account balance 

continue to accrue GIC

be subject to offsetting

no longer require staff to take them off hold unless

progressing collection action

actioning insolvency work prior to non-pursuing as irrecoverable at law

release has been granted.

Taxpayers with debts on hold are no longer:

able to identify their debt is on hold

notified when their debt is placed on hold or taken off hold.

Decisions must be made in accordance with relevant Practice Statements.
Opens in a new window

